    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543052/student-support-widening-participation-officer-indigenous-identified-liverpool-study-hub": {
      "title": "Student Support & Widening Participation Officer (Indigenous Identified)  Liverpool Study Hub",
      "description": "Student Support & Widening Participation Officer (Indigenous Identified)  Liverpool Study Hub Apply now Job no: 543052 Work type: Full Time Location: Other location Categories: Student Services, Student Recruitment / Admissions, Indigenous Employment Type: Full-time contract role until November 2027 (potential for extension) Remuneration: $108k+ 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Liverpool (On-site role) Benefits: Cultural Leave and Indigenous Employee Network This position is open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants only. UNSW has obtained an exemption under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit professional and academic positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander persons only, to fulfil UNSW’s goal of a representative workforce rate. The Liverpool Study Hub is a community-driven space designed to improve tertiary access and boost success for current and future students of any Australian university, TAFE or tertiary provider. Supported by the Australian Government through the Suburban University Study Hubs program , the Hub provides welcoming study spaces, as well as in-person academic, wellbeing, and careers services for students who face increased barriers to study. UNSW is one of the world’s leading universities, committed to a mission to advance Progress for All. Why your Role Matters: As the Student Support & Widening Participation Officer, you will play a key role in strengthening engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and other underrepresented groups to support student participation, retention and success in higher education. Working collaboratively with Hub staff, other educational providers, Gandangara Local Aboriginal Land Council and community organisations, the role builds strong partnerships to bridge gaps in higher education access, connecting prospective and current students with opportunities and support Your day-to-day focus will include: Driving initiatives that improve access, participation and success for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islanders and other underrepresented students in higher education. Delivering culturally responsive support and programs that support current and perspective students’ retention and success. Bringing existing knowledge of higher education pathways to cconnecting students with culturally responsive information, opportunities and referral pathways. Building meaningful community partnerships across schools, education providers and community organisations to champion widening participation and student success Promoting the Liverpool Study Hub through engaging outreach, communications and community engagement activities. Creating a sense of belonging through a culturally safe and inclusive student experience. Who You Are: This position is open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants only. UNSW has obtained an exemption under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit professional and academic positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander persons only, to fulfil UNSW’s goal of a representative workforce rate. Evidence of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander status will be required https://www.indigenous.unsw.edu.au/evidencing-aboriginality-andor-torres-strait-islander-status Bringing a deep understanding of the higher education sector, student lifecycle and the challenges affecting participation and success for students from Indigenous, equity and underrepresented communities, you’ll be confident in developing and delivering student support, outreach or widening participation initiatives that promote access, inclusion, belonging. Key requirements: Relevant degree in education, communications, community services, youth work, marketing or related discipline. Understanding of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures, perspectives, protocols and lived experience. Knowledge of higher education pathways and equity student experiences. Experience delivering student support, outreach or widening participation initiatives. Excellent interpersonal, communication and relationship management. Ability to provide high-quality advisory support, with a commitment to inclusion and culturally responsive practice. Strong organisational, project coordination and digital skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and evaluate activities in a developing environment. Alongside standard pre-employment checks, this role requires the successful candidate to hold a valid paid Working with Children’s Check. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) Establishing your status as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person means any person who: is of Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent; identifies as an Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person; and is accepted as such by the community in which you live and or have lived. All offers of employment will be subject to prior evidencing of Aboriginal/Torres Strait Islander status. For more information on what constitutes as evidencing Aboriginal/Torres Strait Islander status, please email n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: Nat Chenhall – Manager Indigenous Employment n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter addressing the key selection criteria (1 page) before 11.55pm Sunday 27 September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application . Job queries contact: Katherine Molloy, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 14 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543058/research-assistant": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Assistant Apply now Job no: 543058 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Infrastructure, Clinical/Medical Research One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Biotechnology & Biomolecular Science (BABS) teaches undergraduates, mentors postgraduate research students and conducts research in the disciplines of biotechnology, biochemistry, genetics, molecular biology, microbiology, environmental microbiology, medical microbiology and immunology. The Research Assistant in the School of Biotechnology & Biomolecular Sciences at the University of New South Wales provides technical and research assistance to the research team. About the role $92K - $104K plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 6 months Part time (14 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Liaise with stakeholders in the preparation of import permit applications/amendments, biosecurity applications, annual and progress reports. Perform data collection and entry and maintain research databases, specifically for the Ferrari lab database and the biological registers, exercising strong attention to detail and following up discrepancies as required. Provide general administrative support including organisation of and attendance at ARC grant and Ferrari lab meetings. Provide technical support for the cultivation of bacteria in the laboratory, requiring media preparation, culturing and PCR. Respond to routine enquiries relating to research. Undertake other unsupervised research support tasks consistent with established methodologies as needed by the project/s. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A Bachelor of Science, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through a combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated knowledge of and experience with microbiology and genomics. Prior experience supporting a research project and demonstrated knowledge of research protocols. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail and analytical abilities. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Demonstrated ability to make sound judgements. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Belinda Ferrari E: b.ferrari@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 28 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW aspires to be the exemplar Australian university and employer of choice for people from diverse backgrounds. UNSW aims to ensure equality in recruitment, development, retention and promotion of staff and that no-one is disadvantaged on the basis of their gender, cultural background, disability, sexual orientation or identity or Indigenous heritage. We encourage everyone who meets the selection criteria to apply. UNSW partners with Australia’s leading diversity organisations, networks, and campaigns. Please refer to UNSW’s diversity offerings for further information on our flexible work and leave options, and support for carers (childcare, parent rooms, parental leave). Position Description Advertised: 14 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 28 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
