    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542789/senior-student-success-community-development-coordinator": {
      "title": "Senior Student Success & Community Development Coordinator",
      "description": "Senior Student Success & Community Development Coordinator Apply now Job no: 542789 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Student Services Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 7 from $118k + 17% superannuation & leave loading Location:  Kensington NSW (Hybrid Working Arrangements) Why your role matters The Senior Student Success and Community Development Coordinator plays a key role within the Engagement & Student Success team in the Pro Vice-Chancellor Student Success portfolio, contributing to the development and delivery of student success initiatives, including large-scale health and wellbeing campaigns that promote student belonging, wellbeing, engagement and success. The role leads the development, delivery and evaluation of evidence-based and student-informed programs that are impactful, culturally sensitive and appropriately designed for priority cohorts and their peers. Working collaboratively with internal and external stakeholders, Faculties, portfolio and divisional colleagues, and UNSW students, the role applies co-design principles to build students’ awareness, understanding and help-seeking behaviours in relation to their health, wellbeing and overall success. The position also recruits, manages and oversees student casuals, volunteers and peer groups to support the delivery of initiatives and programs. The role reports to the Lead, Student Success, Community & Partnerships and has no direct reports. Specific accountabilities include: Lead the development, coordination and evaluation of evidence-informed programs and activities for UNSW students including campaign communications plans ensuring alignment with UNSW strategy and branding. Coordinate the development and delivery of large-scale health promotion events including Water Safety programs, SEXtember and Mental Health Month. Coordinate the development and delivery of large-scale student success programs, events and initiatives including the annual Belonging Survey, First Year Festival and other identified initiatives. Assist in the development of and oversee the day-to-day operational aspects of student ambassador programs and initiatives. About You: A relevant tertiary qualification and/or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. Demonstrated experience in the development, delivery and evaluation of successful programs to culturally diverse people. Demonstrated experience in leading communications campaigns for diverse youth audiences. Demonstrated experience coordinating large events that engage young people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds. Relevant program or project coordination experience, with the ability to manage conflicting deadlines and multiple priorities. Highly developed interpersonal skills with the ability to work collaboratively using co-design principles and ability to maintain positive and professional relationships with students as well as staff at all levels and internal and external stakeholders. Highly developed written and verbal communication skills, including demonstrated facilitation skills. Ability to work flexibly, productively, and effectively as part of a team while operating autonomously and demonstrating strong initiative and a proactive approach to driving process improvements. The successful candidate will be required to obtain a Paid Working with Children’s Check & a Criminal Record Check. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW . Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Submit your CV & Cover Letter addressing your suitability to the role in relation to the selection criteria (in approximately 2 pages) before 11.55pm Sunday 27th September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Job queries contact: Chloe Gane, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: chloe.gane@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/540724/systems-support-analyst-work-integrated-learning": {
      "title": "Systems Support Analyst - Work Integrated Learning",
      "description": "Systems Support Analyst - Work Integrated Learning Apply now Job no: 540724 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: 18-month Full-Time Fixed Term contract Remuneration:  $108K to $115K depending on experience + 17% super  (level 6) Location: Kensington UNSW Campus (hybrid) Why Your Role Matters The Systems Support Analyst supports the operation and continuous improvement of enterprise systems within the Pro Vice-Chancellor Education & Student Experience portfolio, Experience Pillar, Employability theme. The role is responsible for system support, service desk coordination, configuration and supporting data, reporting and compliance activities. The position acts as a subject matter expert for InPlace and exercises judgement to resolve issues, coordinate escalations, and contribute to process improvements. The role reports to the Lead, Enterprise Systems and Planning and has no direct reports. Key responsibilities include: Oversee service desk operations, including reviewing incoming tickets, triaging and assigning issues based on priority, complexity and team capability, and independently resolving issues. Identify recurring issues and trends, and document and communicate these to stakeholders or escalate to vendors as required Provide subject matter expertise for Employability systems and support stakeholders in system use and best practice Undertake system configuration activities, including implementing and maintaining configurations, supporting new course setup, and translating business requirements into system solutions Perform and monitor data migration activities, ensuring data accuracy and completeness Support compliance activities, including coordinating surveys and providing data for reporting obligations Develop and generate reports and manipulate data (e.g. Excel) to support business and compliance reporting Review system updates, review release notes and communicate relevant changes to stakeholders Monitor system integrations and coordinate issue resolution with UNSW IT and vendors Contribute to process improvements and maintain system documentation Build effective stakeholder relationships and deliver a high level of customer service About you You are a detail-oriented professional who enjoys solving problems, working with enterprise systems and supporting stakeholders in a collaborative, service-focused environment. You will also bring: Demonstrated experience supporting enterprise systems, including configuration and user support. Ability to analyse and resolve system issues and exercise judgement within established frameworks Experience coordinating service desk activities and managing competing priorities Experience developing reports using Microsoft Excel (e.g. pivot tables, lookup functions) Strong organisational, communication, and stakeholder engagement skills Demonstrated initiative and ability to contribute to process improvements Experience in higher education, and/or familiarity with Work Integrated Learning (WIL) and placement systems such as InPlace or Sonia, would be highly regarded. This is an 18-month fixed-term role offering hybrid and flexible working arrangements from Monday to Friday. Please note that this role is not eligible for sponsorship. Application process: Please submit your resume and a cover letter outlining your motivation and suitability for the role and addressing all points under the “About you” section of the job ad. You will also be asked to answer an application question, which you can save and return to before submitting your application. Applications close: 23 September 2026 11.55PM Get in touch for any question or support: Anne de Voronine, Talent Acquisition Associate: a.de_voronine@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 10 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 23 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542835/development-officer-fcp": {
      "title": "Development Officer, FCP",
      "description": "Development Officer, FCP Apply now Job no: 542835 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Fundraising / Philanthropy Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 7 $118,483 - $128,233 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Division of Societal Impact, Equity and Engagement (SIEE) supports a diverse and inclusive staff and student community and works with partner communities and broader society towards positive change. The Development Officer, Foundation & Corporate Philanthropy (FCP) supports the delivery of philanthropic fundraising strategies for the Division, with a focus on cultivating, soliciting and stewarding national and international trusts and foundations. Working collaboratively with development colleagues, academic leaders and stakeholders, the role identifies funding opportunities, develops compelling proposals and builds sustainable relationships that contribute to UNSW’s fundraising priorities and revenue targets. The role reports to the Head of Development (FCP) and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Support the implementation of philanthropic strategies to achieve agreed fundraising priorities, activity targets and revenue goals. Build and manage a personal portfolio of approximately 100 trust and foundation prospects with the capacity or potential to make commitments of $100,000 or more. Work with academic leaders, university partners and development colleagues to identify philanthropic opportunities, develop gift ideas and foster productive relationships. Prepare persuasive EOIs, proposals, solicitation materials, grant reports and cultivation communications for trust and foundation donors and prospects. Develop and maintain effective cultivation and stewardship plans for prospects and donors. Provide high-quality fundraising advice, information and support to stakeholders and contribute to a coordinated whole-of-University approach. Develop and implement an annual work plan supporting agreed prospect engagement, activity and fundraising goals. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant degree with extensive experience in fundraising and donor relations, preferably in a higher education setting, including writing grant applications and reports. Demonstrated ability to articulate a compelling theory of change and secure major gifts, pledges or commitments of $100,000 or greater. Excellent stakeholder management skills, with the ability to establish collaborative and sustainable relationships with alumni, prospects, donors, colleagues, academic leaders and other UNSW stakeholders. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to write persuasively and present complex information clearly through applications, briefings, proposals and written presentations. Superior organisational skills, high attention to detail and proven ability to establish and realise objectives in a fast-paced environment. Strong knowledge of fundraising principles and tax laws that impact charitable giving, personal assets and estates. Substantial experience working effectively in a large complex organisation, with demonstrated ability to influence, build support and show initiative, creativity and independent judgement. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV and a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role in relation to the selection criteria (listed under ‘Skills & Experience’ in the Position Description) via the application portal. Applications close: 11:55pm on 23 September A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 10 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 23 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543012/executive-assistant": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant",
      "description": "Executive Assistant Apply now Job no: 543012 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-Term Contract until September 2027 Remuneration: Level 7 $118,483 - $128,233 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Office of the CFO provides governance, strategic, operational and executive level support to the Chief Financial Officer and the UNSW Finance Branch. As Executive Assistant, you play a pivotal role in supporting the Chief Financial Officer while providing broad administrative and operational support across UNSW Finance. You help ensure the Finance team is responsive to the needs of a diverse range of stakeholders by coordinating priorities, managing complex schedules, and facilitating effective stakeholder engagement. Through proactive planning, strong relationship management and attention to detail, you contribute to the smooth operation of the Office of the CFO and support the successful delivery of business objectives. The position reports to the Executive Officer and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Provide superior executive and administrative support to the Chief Financial Officer (CFO). Manage a complex diary and prioritise key appointments, meetings, correspondence, preparation time, conflicting deadlines and reporting timelines for the CFO. Manage day-to-day operational matters for the Office of the CFO, including reviewing and redirecting matters, sourcing and preparing background information, and drafting correspondence, briefing notes, minutes, reports and presentations. Provide proactive executive support while exercising discretion, initiative and confidentiality at all times. Manage administrative tasks for Finance, including purchasing, onboarding activities, EM and IT service requests, file and system access, and shared Finance resources such as space, stationery and printers. Drive, coordinate and execute projects, initiatives and other ad hoc programs on behalf of the CFO or as part of the Office of the CFO team, including events such as planning days, team building activities and Town Halls. Coordinate training and workshops across Finance, liaising with internal and external stakeholders on preparation, delivery and evaluation. Interpret and advise on University policy and governance frameworks, and coordinate executive support for key Committee and Steerco meetings chaired by the CFO, including technology requirements for virtual staff engagement sessions. Develop and maintain effective relationships and networks with internal and external stakeholders, acting as a professional and trusted link between the CFO and key stakeholders. Work closely with the Office of the CFO team to develop and oversee administrative practices, systems and procedures that optimise efficiency and support quality outcomes. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant tertiary qualifications and/or equivalent level of knowledge gained through a combination of education, training and/or experience in a similar role. Extensive experience supporting senior executives, with significant expertise providing high quality operational and management support at executive level. Highly developed organisational skills and the ability to work flexibly and proactively, identify priorities, manage competing deadlines and maintain exceptional attention to detail. Demonstrated ability to identify, analyse and solve administrative problems, summarise key issues, develop innovative solutions and deliver high-level outcomes autonomously and as part of a team. Strong influencing and negotiation skills, with the ability to build effective relationships and secure resources from diverse internal and external stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to excellent customer service, with a positive, flexible and adaptable approach and willingness to take on a wide range of tasks in a changing environment. Strong interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to establish and maintain effective professional relationships. Proven high-quality experience in report preparation and presentations, with the ability to communicate complex information in an accessible manner. Demonstrated high-level project coordination skills and the ability to influence and work with senior management and stakeholders to achieve successful outcomes. Working experience with Office 365, including Teams. Capacity and willingness to work effectively both autonomously and as part of a collaborative and multi-faceted team. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV and a cover letter outlining your suitability for the role via the application portal before 11:30pm on 21 September. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 10 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 21 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543013/senior-group-reporting-consolidation-analyst": {
      "title": "Senior Group Reporting & Consolidation Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Group Reporting & Consolidation Analyst Apply now Job no: 543013 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Finance, Analyst Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 9: $153,933 - $163,338 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Finance work environment is dynamic and collaborative, with a strong focus on service quality, continuous improvement and career development. Financial Planning and Analysis oversees budget setting, forecasting and management reporting processes to accurately monitor the financial performance of UNSW Sydney. As the Senior Group Reporting & Consolidation Analyst, you will provide management reporting and analysis, including balance sheet and cashflow reviews to track financial performance across key indicators. You will translate complex data and analytical findings into meaningful advice and guidance that supports senior management decision-making and University objectives. The role works to time-sensitive reporting deadlines, with occasional out-of-hours and weekend work required during busy periods. The position reports to the Senior Manager Group Reporting & Consolidation and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Prepare and distribute month-end and year-end business unit reporting packs and maintain Parent and Group consolidated analysis and reports. Review and analyse the financial performance, expenditure and position of UNSW Sydney’s parent and controlled entities, identifying drivers of variations and initiating remedial action where required. Act as a key point of contact for Finance Business Partners, Finance Managers and Analysts, collaborating to ensure timely inputs and accurate, high-quality information and commentary for stakeholders. Own end-to-end preparation of Parent and Group reporting across relevant areas, including fixed assets, financial assets and liabilities, restricted cash, internal eliminations and headcount reporting. Prepare insightful variance analysis and translate findings into meaningful business advice for senior management. Undertake balance sheet and cashflow reporting and analysis, including review monthly variances and undertake ad-hoc analysis of material movements. Ensure the accuracy and integrity of data prior to submission to senior management. Drive efficiencies and quality improvements in management reporting, including contributing to business intelligence dashboard reporting and process enhancements. Liaise with finance colleagues, Financial Control & Compliance, treasury, faculties, divisions and controlled entities to facilitate information flow and manage stakeholder needs. Coach and mentor more junior staff to build capability, understanding and productivity. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline, with a CA or CPA qualification (or equivalent). Substantial experience in management reporting, budgeting and forecasting in a large commercial organisation, or a similar group or divisional financial planning and analysis or management reporting role. Proven skills in developing financial forecasting models integrating P&L, balance sheet and cashflow statements. Well-developed computer and business intelligence expertise, including proficiency in Excel (including data visualisation), PowerPoint and Word. Experience with Calumo, TM1, Hyperion Essbase, Power BI or similar tools will be highly regarded. Demonstrable working knowledge of Australian Accounting Standards. Ability to rapidly absorb information and analyse, interpret and integrate ambiguous and complex data. Excellent attention to detail and ability to deliver results within required timetable and quality parameters. Highly developed written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills, with demonstrated experience working with a broad range of stakeholders and customers. Self-motivation, intellectual curiosity and a strong drive for continuous improvement. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your CV and cover letter outlining your suitability for the position in relation to the selection criteria (found under ‘skills & experience” on the PD) online via our application portal. Applications close: 9am on 24 September A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. * UNSW is unable to offer sponsorship for this position. Applicants must have full Australian working rights for the duration of the contract. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 10 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 24 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/541764/technical-assistant": {
      "title": "Technical Assistant",
      "description": "Technical Assistant Apply now Job no: 541764 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Laboratory Technician Technical Assistant Employment Type: Continuing, Full - time (35 hours a week) Remuneration : $84,588-$89,230 base, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: Kensington, Sydney About UNSW: UNSW Medicine is a national leader in learning teaching and research, with close affiliations to a number of Australia’s finest hospitals, research institutes and health care organisations. The Medicine & Health Research Infrastructure and Operational Services (MEDRIOS) Team, led by the Research Infrastructure Manager, supports over 650 researchers across the UNSW Kensington, Randwick and St. George Clinical and Sutherland Campuses. Research and technical support services are shared between buildings and off-campus UNSW-managed facilities to provide logistics support, effective laboratory infrastructure, equipment maintenance and media preparation services as part of the larger UNSW Biomedical Precinct. Details of the role The Technical Assistant provides routine support to UNSW Medicine & Health researchers in regard to facilities, resources and general laboratory housekeeping at both on-campus and off campus locations within UNSW-managed facilities. Join a collaborative technical services team supporting world-class research across multiple laboratory environments. This role is ideal for someone who enjoys hands-on work, takes pride in maintaining high laboratory standards, and thrives in a service-focused environment. You will contribute to the smooth operation of research facilities through equipment maintenance, laboratory support, media preparation, waste management, testing services, and logistics coordination. Working closely with researchers and technical staff, you will play an important role in ensuring facilities are safe, efficient, and well maintained, helping research teams focus on delivering high-impact outcomes. The role of Technical Assistant reports to the Technical Team Leader and has no direct reports. This position involves a moderate level of physical activity. The successful candidate will be required to safely perform manual handling tasks, including pushing, pulling and moving equipment and materials of up to 10 kg and also possess a valid driver’s licence. Some key skills required: Completion of Year 10 or 12 and relevant experience, or completion of a Certificate or Associate Diploma in a relevant discipline, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. Assist with the day-to-day operations of the Faculty’s research laboratories under the guidance and direction of the Technical Team Leader. Maintain laboratory equipment and ensure regular and systematic maintenance is carried out where required. Ensure all equipment, instruments and glassware washing, autoclaving, disinfecting, and cleaning is conducted to a high standard and in accordance with directions. Dispose of hazardous and non-hazardous waste, including biomedical waste, in accordance with relevant processes, procedures and legislation. Prepare media and solutions for purchase by all researchers across the University through UNSW Stores, as required. Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a team environment as well as independently, including under direction when performing more complex tasks. Well-developed interpersonal and communication skills, with proven ability to provide high level customer service to a wide range of stakeholders of various backgrounds and levels of seniority. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Medical fitness check (this role requires lifting of goods up to a weight of 10 kgs) Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Wednesday 16th September before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542513/postdoctoral-fellow-quality-use-of-medicines": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Fellow - Quality Use of Medicines",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Fellow - Quality Use of Medicines Apply now Job no: 542513 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate, Senior Research Associate Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 12 months Remuneration: Level A: $95,036 - $126,711 + 17% super + leave loading Work rights: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Post-Doctoral Fellow plays a critical role in supporting research activities across a portfolio of projects focused on the Quality Use of Medicines (QUM). Working closely with the project team, the role contributes to the design, implementation and evaluation of qualitative and quantitative research, supporting the generation of evidence to inform policy, practice, and improved medicines use. The Post-Doctoral Fellow will collaborate with multidisciplinary teams, assist in coordinating research activities and contribute to knowledge translation efforts that enhance the impact and sustainability of the QUM program. This position is embedded within a nationally recognised research team focused on improving the safety and quality use of medicines, with collaboration across UNSW’s National Drug and Alcohol Research Centre, School of Clinical Medicine and School of Health Sciences. The role reports to the program’s Chief Investigators and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Conduct and coordinate research activities to the highest scientific and ethical standards. Contribute to the conceptual development of research questions and study designs under the guidance of a research leader, aligning with the program’s strategic goals. Conduct comprehensive literature reviews to identify current evidence, methodological approaches and knowledge gaps relevant to the Quality Use of Medicines, and use findings to inform study design, analysis and interpretation. Undertake data collection, data management and data analysis, including application of advanced statistical techniques. Prepare scientific papers, reports and conference abstracts, and present research findings at meetings, workshops and conferences. Contribute to stakeholder engagement and support knowledge translation by collaborating with research partners, participating in dissemination activities and promoting the application of research findings to relevant audiences. Assist in the preparation of research grant proposals and tenders for commissioned research. Independently plan workload and manage timelines to meet project milestones, and assist with HDR student supervision where required. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: A PhD degree in pharmacy, medicine, nursing, public health, or a related discipline. Demonstrated ability to conduct comprehensive literature reviews to identify current evidence, methodological approaches and knowledge gaps, and to synthesise findings to inform study design, analysis and interpretation. Demonstrated experience in designing and conducting qualitative and quantitative research studies. Proven ability to manage and analyse qualitative and/or quantitative data, using statistical software such as NVivo, SPSS, SAS or Stata. Excellent written communication skills, including the ability to prepare peer-reviewed publications, policy briefs and plain-language summaries for diverse audiences. Excellent verbal communication and interpersonal skills, with a demonstrated ability to collaborate with multidisciplinary teams and engage with stakeholders such as clinicians, policymakers and consumer groups. Strong understanding of ethical principles, data governance and privacy requirements in health research, with experience contributing to ethics applications. Experience in preparing research grant proposals and contributing to funding applications. Ability to independently manage workload, prioritise tasks and meet deadlines within project timelines and budget constraints. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Please submit your CV along with a cover letter that addresses your suitability for the position in relation to each of the selection criteria (listed in the ‘Skills & Experience’ section of the PD) via the application portal. Applications close: 11:30pm on 22 September A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, please contact Merita O’Connell, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: merita.oconnell@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 22 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542597/research-program-manager": {
      "title": "Research Program Manager",
      "description": "Research Program Manager Apply now Job no: 542597 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management, Clinical/Medical Research Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term until 31 January 2028 with the possibility of extension Remuneration: Level 8: $132,445 - $149,228 + 17% Super + Leave Loading Work rights: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: UNSW Randwick About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Research Program Manager plays a strategic and operational leadership role in the planning, development and delivery of a multi-centre portfolio of research projects focused on the quality use of medicines (QUM). This role supports the day-to-day running of projects, research strategy, manages funding applications and grants, ensures compliance with ethics and regulatory requirements, and facilitates knowledge translation. The Research Program Manager coordinates multidisciplinary teams and collaborations, monitors research outputs and impact, and contributes to the overall success and sustainability of the research program. The role reports to the program Chief Investigators and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Support research strategy and development to advance the program’s objectives and long-term impact. Manage research funding applications and grants, including drafting documentation, coordinating input from collaborators and ensuring alignment with funding body requirements. Ensure compliance with relevant governance, ethics and regulatory frameworks applicable to the conduct of research projects. Coordinate multidisciplinary research teams and collaborations, including researchers, clinicians, policy experts and administrative staff. Undertake day-to-day project management by leading the planning, execution and delivery of research projects, ensuring alignment with strategic objectives and timelines. Lead project governance activities and maintain project documentation including project plans, risk registers, stakeholder maps, budgets and progress reports. Manage project risks, issues, change requests and financials, implementing mitigation strategies and ensuring project resilience. Monitor research progress and performance against milestones, budgets and deliverables, and provide regular updates to investigator teams and funding bodies. Perform data collection, management and analysis to support research objectives. Facilitate effective stakeholder engagement across clinical, academic and policy sectors, ensuring transparent communication and collaboration. Oversee quality assurance processes and support dissemination of project findings through reports, presentations and publications, contributing to knowledge translation and policy influence. Monitor research outputs and impact and represent the research program across UNSW work areas, advisory groups, and at local and national meetings; lead and manage other program-wide research activities as required. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant Master’s level tertiary qualification with substantial experience in managing research projects, or equivalent competence gained through a combination of education, training and experience. Proven experience in research project management, with a strong understanding of the full project lifecycle, including strategic planning, execution, monitoring and reporting. Demonstrated ability to support research strategy and development, including contributions to funding applications and grant management. Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail in documentation, reporting and stakeholder communications. Proven ability to support knowledge translation and dissemination through publications, presentations and stakeholder engagement. Experience working collaboratively with diverse teams, including researchers, clinicians, policy experts and administrative staff, across academic and external environments. Familiarity with ethics and regulatory compliance processes relevant to health and medical research. Sound knowledge of budgeting, financial management and resource allocation procedures within a research context. Experience in data collection, management and analysis to support research outcomes. High-level proficiency in using a range of computer systems and applications, including Microsoft Word, Excel and project management tools. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines using judgement and initiative. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, while also taking initiative and working independently when managing competing demands. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV and a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role in relation to the selection criteria (listed under ‘Skills & Experience’ in the Position Description) via the application portal. Applications close: 11:30pm on 22 September A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Merita O’Connell, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: merita.oconnell@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 22 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542670/project-managerconduct-and-integrity": {
      "title": "Project Manager-Conduct and Integrity",
      "description": "Project Manager-Conduct and Integrity Apply now Job no: 542670 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management, Governance, Policy & Compliance Employment Type: Full- time, Continuing role Remuneration: $132k-$149k + 17% Super & leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid work arrangement available) About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. This role sits within Conduct and Integrity, which handles serious complaints, misconduct, investigation issues, and providing education and prevention strategies to foster respect and integrity across UNSW. Why your Role Matters This is more than a project management role. It’s an opportunity to help shape how UNSW listens, responds, learns and improves when complaints and misconduct arise. Working within Conduct and Integrity, you’ll help turn policy and process reform into practical improvements that strengthen trust, fairness and accountability across the University. Reporting to the Deputy Director, you’ll work with stakeholders across UNSW to deliver projects that improve complaints management, investigations and integrity-related practices. If you enjoy bringing clarity to complexity, building strong relationships and delivering meaningful change, this role offers the opportunity to make a real impact. Skills & Experience summary: You will bring practical project and change management experience across policy and compliance settings and can demonstrate: Demonstrated project and change management experience, alongside Prince2 certification, Australian Institute of Project Management or equivalent. Ability to engage and build and manage professional relationships with multiple stakeholders with often competing priorities. An advisory approach with strong communication and influencing skills to implement changes and process improvements. High emotional intelligence to advise on sensitive matters. Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Strong research, business and data analysis skills, with the ability to translate and communicate complex concepts in a simple way. Demonstrated success working autonomously and effectively on initiatives with a range of people at different levels within a service delivery unit and across an organisation. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the position description found on Jobs@UNSW. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before Sunday 27 September by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Katherine Molloy, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542712/project-officer-community-engagement-identified": {
      "title": "Project Officer (Community Engagement) – Identified",
      "description": "Project Officer (Community Engagement) – Identified Apply now Job no: 542712 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management, Indigenous Fixed Term – 24 months Full Time (35-hour week) Remuneration Level 6: $108,021 - $115,233 + 17% Superannuation Kensington, NSW (on campus requirement with potential hybrid available) Why This Role Matters: The Project Officer (Community Engagement) addresses barriers to access and participation experienced by underrepresented cohorts, taking an intersectional approach to supporting engagement, pathways and success in UNSW Engineering. This role will work in collaboration with the Program Manager (Student Gender Equity) who focuses on women, non-binary and gender diverse students, the Community Engagement Coordinator, and the Senior Program Officer (Indigenous Engagement), as well as Future Student Recruitment and other areas of the Faculty of Engineering. As an Identified role, the role will also include the engagement in meaningful partnerships with Indigenous communities. The role entails providing project coordination and support, reporting and analytical support, developing communications, maintaining key internal and external relationships, and providing administrative support for key programs and projects. The role is an identified Indigenous position. UNSW considers that being Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander is a genuine occupational qualification under s.14 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW). This position is open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants only. UNSW has obtained an exemption under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit professional and academic positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander persons only, to fulfil UNSW’s goal of a representative workforce rate. Accountabilities: (please review the Position Description for a full list of Accountabilities) Support the delivery of community engagement initiatives and activities targeting cohorts currently underrepresented in the engineering sector, including Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander People; women, non-binary and gender diverse people; students from a low SES background, and those located in remote/rural areas. Support the development of project plans and schedules and participate in planning processes for key initiatives and activities including, but not limited to, those associated with engineering Indigenous engagement, student gender equity programming, widening participation programming and UNSW Makerbus activities. Monitor, track and report on the status of project deliverables to ensure time, cost and quality metrics are in line with approved project plans for assigned projects. Provide practical and proactive project support and administrative services to project teams and stakeholders. Coordinate project administration including coordination of workshops and meetings, manage assigned actions and follow-ups and project status reporting. Develop and manage effective communications with key stakeholders, both internal and external to the project, including Nura Gili: Centre for Indigenous Programs, UNSW Makerbus, Future Students, Student Equity, high schools & primary schools, Faculty partners, and student societies. Support program implementation under the direction of the Program Manager (Student Equity), with guidance from the Program Manager (Student Gender Equity), Senior Program Officer (Indigenous Engagement) and Community Engagement Coordinator. Skills and Experience: To be considered you will hold Australian Working Rights or Australian Citizenship. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. (please review the Position Description for a full list of Skills and Experience) This is an identified position, and applicants must be an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. Confirmation of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander status will be required. Knowledge of NSW School curricula, specifically NESA (NSW Education Standards Authority) and links to STEM subjects. Knowledge and experience in Indigenous engagement is highly desirable. Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Sound stakeholder management skills, with the ability to liaise effectively with a range of stakeholders. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Project experience, with an understanding of the project life cycle and development of project plans, objectives and documentation. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Pre-employment checks required for this position: Provide Evidencing Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander documentation, if successful. Working with Children Check Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including:​ 10 days paid cultural leave per year Indigenous Employee Network 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: Submit a Cover Letter (no more than 2 pages) and CV online via the UNSW Job Board before Wednesday 30 th September 2026 by 11:30pm. Get in Touch: Any applications sent to the contacts below will not be accepted. If you any questions about the role or recruitment process, please contact Allyssar Hamoud at a.hamoud@unsw.edu.au If you are successful, you'll will be asked to provide a confirmation of Aboriginality. If you need assistance with this, please get in touch with Natalie Chenhall at n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 30 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542819/impact-compliance-officer": {
      "title": "Impact & Compliance Officer",
      "description": "Impact & Compliance Officer Apply now Job no: 542819 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Governance, Policy & Compliance Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 6 from $108k + 17% superannuation & leave loading Location:  Kensington NSW (Hybrid Working Arrangements) Why your role matters The Impact & Compliance Officer supports governance, legislative compliance and reporting activities across the Pro Vice-Chancellor Student Success portfolio’s student support initiatives, boards and committees. The role provides advice to stakeholders on governance matters and contributes to projects focused on legislative requirements, compliance and impact reporting. The position also supports the development and implementation of strategic and operational plans, policies and procedures across the Engagement & Student Success team and broader portfolio. The role reports to the Manager, Student Governance & Projects and has no direct reports. Specific accountabilities include: Collect, prepare and distribute the agenda and documentation for each committee preparing briefing papers for stakeholder engagement as required. Attend meetings, prepare minutes and take appropriate action to enable the timely dissemination of meeting outcomes, maintaining accurate records in accordance with legislative requirements. Liaise with committee members, senior management and other members of the portfolio and University Governance office as required to facilitate the effective operation of committees, including establishing meeting schedules and action plans. Maintain and assist in the development of documentation required for the effective project management and evaluation of student support initiatives and policy implementation, including coordinating workshops, surveys and engagement polls to effectively garner student and staff feedback. About You: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated understanding and application of information organisation and retrieval. Ability to research issues appropriately and report findings accurately, both verbally and in writing, using data visualisation tools. Demonstrated ability to operate with a high level of confidentiality, diplomacy and discretion. Strong communication and interpersonal skills and the ability to enable professional interaction as well as build and maintain positive relationships with colleagues, partners and stakeholders both internal and external. Demonstrated experience in the planning and coordination of projects and the successful delivery of programs. Excellent written and verbal communication skills and demonstrated ability to create accurate records of meetings, with a high level of attention to detail. Excellent organisational skills with the ability to manage and resolve conflicting priorities; ability to take responsibility for own work and to appropriately refer problems. Advanced level of computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications, systems and databases, including demonstrated ability to work with information systems and databases and ability to learn and implement new systems. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW . Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Submit your CV & Cover Letter addressing your suitability to the role in relation to the selection criteria (in approximately 2 pages) before 11.55pm Tuesday 22nd September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Job queries contact: Chloe Gane, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: chloe.gane@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 22 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542827/marketing-digital-experience-faculty-partner": {
      "title": "Marketing & Digital Experience Faculty Partner",
      "description": "Marketing & Digital Experience Faculty Partner Apply now Job no: 542827 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Marketing / Communications Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed Term Contract until Sep 2028 Remuneration: Competitive Remuneration Package – Senior Appointment Location:  Kensington NSW (Hybrid Working Arrangements) Why your role matters The Marketing & Digital Experience (M&DX) team at UNSW strengthens student recruitment by positioning the University as the preferred choice for local and international students, while enhancing its global brand reputation through distinctive marketing and the promotion of UNSW’s world-renowned expertise. The M&DX Faculty Partner is responsible for optimising the educational value proposition and program portfolio of an allocated business unit. The role shapes initiatives for local and international markets to improve program competitiveness, support student volume, quality and diversity outcomes, and identify opportunities to develop new programs in response to emerging demand. Acting as the primary connection between the business unit and UNSW’s central marketing, student recruitment and digital experience functions, the role provides strategic advice, builds trusted relationships with senior stakeholders and balances business unit priorities with the broader interests of the University. The M&DX Faculty Partner drives outcomes, manages projects and coordinates activity across Faculties by drawing on subject matter expertise from teams throughout UNSW. The role reports to the Team Lead, M&DX Faculty Partners, has no direct reports and provides indirect leadership to three to four Senior Recruitment Product Managers and Faculty Recruitment Officers. Specific accountabilities include: Lead the development of the assigned Faculty / Business Unit learner recruitment strategy (inclusive of LLL, TNE, non-award, B2B/B2G etc.)  in line with the overarching Faculty strategy and UNSW Marketing & Digital Experience strategy, incorporating achievement of student load plan targets and strategic initiatives. Act as a trusted advisor to the Business Leadership (Dean, FED, Associate Deans, Heads of School, etc.) in matters related to educational value proposition, product strategy and portfolio management, student recruitment and marketing, branding and digital experience to identify critical ways in which the faculty can achieve domestic and international commercial outcomes. Responsible for shaping and optimising the Faculty product portfolio to improve portfolio market share and maximise new demand opportunities across domestic and international markets, including oversight of new program development, market research and go to market launch, core program benchmarking, competitive monitoring and recommendations for remedial program positioning. About You: A relevant degree with extensive experience in senior business partnering/ stakeholder management roles or an equivalent level of industry knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training and experience. Outstanding relationship management skills, with a demonstrable track record of fostering and maintaining strong and successful internal and external networks, supporting successful organisational outcomes. Strong commercial acumen and an ability to set targets, plan complex work plans and review return on investment to achieve business outcomes. Demonstrated success in designing and delivering sales and marketing strategies that achieve business outcomes for complex product/ service portfolios. Proven track record in managing complex projects with high-level critical thinking and analytical skills plus the ability to distil complex information and finalise development objectives. Demonstrated ability to provide strategic advice and execute strategy on behalf of Senior Executives and to influence, as appropriate, to ensure successful outcomes. Successful record of managing multiple, complex initiatives, coupled with a genuine passion for customer service. University and Faculty experience is highly desired. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW . Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Submit your CV & Cover Letter addressing your suitability to the role in relation to the selection criteria (in approximately 2 pages) before 11.55pm Wednesday 23rd September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Job queries contact: Chloe Gane, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: chloe.gane@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 23 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542851/advice-night-administrator-kingsford-legal-centre-unsw": {
      "title": "Advice Night Administrator – Kingsford Legal Centre UNSW",
      "description": "Advice Night Administrator – Kingsford Legal Centre UNSW Apply now Job no: 542851 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Legal Advice Night Administrator – Kingsford Legal Centre UNSW Employment Type: Part Time, 16 hours per week (Monday to Thursday 3.30pm - 7.30pm) Duration: Fixed Term Contract until 31 December 2027 Remuneration: Level 5 from $92,265 - $104,401 (pro rata) + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About Us: Kingsford Legal Centre (KLC) is a community legal centre providing free legal advice and assistance to our local community and to people across New South Wales. Our team of solicitors provide advice on matters such as Domestic Violence, Credit and Debt, Victim’s Compensation, Criminal Law, Employment Law, and Discrimination Law. Why Your Role Matters: The Advice Night Administrator provides effective administrative support to the Monday to Thursday evening advice sessions within Kingsford Legal Centre to ensure our services are provided efficiently to meet the operating needs of KLC. This role supports KLC lawyers, volunteers and students in the delivery of high-quality legal advice in our evening clinics. The role reports to the Centre’s Operations Manager supporting the Kingsford Legal Centre Director and Deputy. The role works across advice clinics which take place Monday to Thursday evenings, and the ordinary hours are 3:30pm to 7:30pm. Key Responsibilities: Provide high level administrative support to the Kingsford Legal Centre evening advice night sessions Act as the first point of contact for clients and students, maintaining effective channels of communication, to ensure the effective operation of the evening client advice session Provide effective specialist support and induction relating to administrative guidelines, processes and activities relating to legal service delivery, client document management and other KLC systems to students and volunteer solicitors Respond to a range of in-person, telephone and email enquiries from internal and external stakeholders, and using sound judgment, initiate, action or escalate as necessary relating to the evening advice session Undertake induction for Lawyers, Ethics and Justice students, attending the evening advice session, including WHS requirements and undertake administrative set up for the night Participate in relevant projects and business improvement activities to streamline processes and facilitate administrative efficiency. Skills and Experience: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience Advanced time management, organisational and problem-solving skills, including the ability to meet deadlines Well-developed interpersonal written and verbal communication skills Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels Highly proficient computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications / client database systems Ability to make sound judgements and work both independently and as part of a team Demonstrated experience supporting and contributing to projects and initiatives Demonstrated ability to guide, support and supervise students. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. It's our people who make UNSW one of the world's leading universities. Along with the opportunity to work on diverse, high-risk projects that support world-class research and education, you'll enjoy benefits that are hard to find in many construction and infrastructure environments, including: 17% superannuation and additional leave loading. Additional 3 days' leave over the Christmas shutdown. Career development and learning opportunities. Eligible for community services industry portable long service leave scheme. Discounts and entitlements across retail, education and fitness. To learn more about working at UNSW, visit: https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: Submit your CV & a cover letter briefly addressing the skills and experience bullet points listed in the position description by 11.55pm Wednesday 23 rd September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Contact: Amy Denney a.denney@unsw.edu.au - Talent Acquisition Partner Please apply directly through the website , applications sent to the contact above will not be accepted. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role . UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 23 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542995/team-leader-general-administration-unsw-engineering-school-shared-services": {
      "title": "Team Leader – General Administration (UNSW Engineering School Shared Services)",
      "description": "Team Leader – General Administration (UNSW Engineering School Shared Services) Apply now Job no: 542995 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Management The Opportunity: The Team Leader, General Administration is responsible for leading and managing administrative services and the general administration team within the Faculty of Engineering’s eight Schools. The role reports to the Deputy Faculty Executive Director with a dotted reporting line to the School Managers and has a direct report. Salary, Level 7: $118,483 per annum + 17% superannuation Full Time: 35 hours Fixed Term to September 2027 Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia (on campus attendance 4 days a week) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. But what makes us different isn’t only what we do, it’s how we do it. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Faculty of Engineering at UNSW continues to be a national and international leader in engineering research and education, cementing its status as the University’s premier research faculty. As the largest Engineering Faculty in Australia, it graduates the highest number of engineers and maintains the largest research expenditure in the country. Through excellence in both fundamental and applied research, the Faculty strives to sustain and further its global impact, aiming to be ranked among the top engineering faculties worldwide. For more information, visit the Faculty website: https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/about-us Skills & Experience: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Excellent time management and organisational skills, and the ability to work well under pressure and manage multiple tasks with competing deadlines. Demonstrated ability to supervise, influence and guide staff. Highly advanced interpersonal, written and oral communication skills. Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. Highly proficient computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications. Demonstrated project management experience with demonstrated ability to act with a high level of independence, initiative, problem solving, analysis, and judgment, providing accurate and appropriate outcomes. Excellent numeracy skills with the ability to understand and communicate financial information and monitor budgets. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for these positions can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW . To Apply: Please click the apply now button and submit your CV, Cover Letter and Responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: Allyssar Hamoud – Talent Acquisition Associate E: a.hamoud@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Wednesday 23 rd September 2026 UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 23 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543072/manager-research-training-culture-arc-centre-of-excellence-for-quantum-computer-performance-and-integration": {
      "title": "Manager, Research Training & Culture  - ARC Centre of Excellence for Quantum Computer Performance and Integration",
      "description": "Manager, Research Training & Culture  - ARC Centre of Excellence for Quantum Computer Performance and Integration Apply now Job no: 543072 Work type: Full Time, Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Infrastructure The Opportunity The ARC Centre of Excellence for Quantum Computer Performance and Integration (QPI), based at UNSW, has an opportunity for a Manager, Research Training & Culture . QPI is a multi-institutional research initiative spanning eight Australian university nodes and working with government, research organisations and industry partners to advance useful quantum computing while building the capability and culture that will shape Australia’s quantum future. Alongside its research objectives, QPI is committed to developing the next generation of quantum research leaders, embedding equity and inclusion across the Centre, and fostering a culture of scientific integrity and collaborative practice. In this role, you will lead an integrated research training and capability development program for HDR students and postdoctoral researchers, while coordinating mentoring, leadership development, recruitment and onboarding initiatives, and using culture and performance data to inform continuous improvement. To be successful in this role, you will bring demonstrated experience designing and delivering training, professional development or capability-building programs, together with strong program, project and event management skills. You will be an accomplished relationship builder who can work effectively across multiple institutions, committees and stakeholder groups, and bring sound judgement, strong communication skills and a genuine commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. Experience working in a multi-institutional research environment, supporting HDR students and early career researchers, or contributing to mentoring, recruitment or culture initiatives will be highly regarded. This role reports to the Chief Operating Officer and has no direct reports. Salary, Level 8: AUD $132,445 to $149,228 per annum + 17% superannuation Employment type: Full time or Part time Appointment type: Fixed-term contract – 3 years Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia Travel: Interstate travel will be required to visit QPI’s other university nodes across Australia. About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 50 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. About QPI The ARC Centre of Excellence for Quantum Computer Performance and Integration (QPI) is a newly established national research initiative bringing together leading scientists, engineers, students and industry partners to accelerate breakthroughs in quantum computing. Hosted at UNSW Sydney within the Faculty of Engineering, QPI spans eight Australian university nodes: UNSW, the University of Sydney, the University of Technology Sydney, the Australian National University, Griffith University, the University of Queensland, RMIT University and the University of Melbourne. The Centre also works with government agencies, research organisations and industry partners across the quantum computing sector. The Centre is led by a Director and three Deputy Directors, each responsible for one of QPI’s priority areas: Research Excellence, People & Culture, and Innovation & Translation. For more information on the Centre, visit: https://www.arc.gov.au/arc-centre-excellence-quantum-computer-performance-and-integration Skills and Experience A relevant degree with subsequent relevant experience in an educational institution or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and training. Demonstrated experience designing, coordinating or delivering training, professional development or capability-building programs. Proven program, project and event management skills, including budget management, program monitoring and evaluation. Demonstrated ability to lead initiatives and competently manage workload, changing priorities and peak periods. Strong written, verbal and interpersonal skills, with the ability to build effective working relationships across multiple institutions and through committee and governance structures. Initiative, sound judgement and the ability to develop practical solutions to problems. Ability to work independently while contributing effectively to a geographically distributed team. Demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion, and to fostering a respectful, inclusive workplace culture. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Desirable Experience working within a multi-institutional research collaboration. Familiarity with the Higher Degree Research environment and the career development needs of HDR students and Early Career Researchers. Experience supporting structured mentoring, recruitment or supervisory arrangements (such as hiring panels, supervisory panels or buddy/mentoring schemes). Demonstrated experience contributing to equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives, including designing or interpreting culture surveys and tracking progress against agreed metrics. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for this position can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW. We recognise that there is no single pathway to success in this role. We welcome applicants with experience gained in universities, research organisations, industry, government or the not-for-profit sector. We value backgrounds spanning researcher development, education, people and culture, program delivery, stakeholder engagement and community building. If you meet most of the criteria and believe your experience could contribute to QPI’s success, we encourage you to apply. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offers a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including:​ 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working/Hybrid working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. To Apply: Please click the “Apply Now” button and submit your CV and Cover Letter Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. To be considered, applicants must have full working rights in Australia and be able to work on-site at our Kensington campus. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: For role-specific inquiries, please contact Monica Brockmyre (Chief Operating Officer) E: m.brockmyre@unsw.edu.au For questions regarding the recruitment process, please contact Eugene Aves (Talent Acquisition Partner) E: eugene.aves@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Sunday, 27 September 2026 As part of our recruitment process, candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/525902/scientist-rna-institute": {
      "title": "Scientist - RNA Institute",
      "description": "Scientist - RNA Institute Apply now Job no: 525902 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Administration support, Laboratory Technician, Research Infrastructure One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The UNSW RNA Institute is Australia’s leading RNA research institute, bringing together researchers from UNSW Science, Engineering and Medicine, led by Professor Pall Thordarson, with collaborative input from other universities and institutes. Our mission is to grow and support the NSW RNA Ecosystem: mRNA Vaccines Other RNA Therapeutics RNA Delivery Systems RNA Technologies RNA Education RNA Research The Scientist carries out research, development and production of RNA and related products within the UNSW RNA Institute. The role will contribute and apply knowledge in the field of RNA processing to a range of projects conducted within the Institute. About the role Level 5 ($92K - $104k) Level 6 ($108k - $115K) plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 14 months (to December 31, 2027) Full-time (35 hours per week) The role reports to the Senior Scientist – pDNA, mRNA and has no direct reports. Specific responsibilities for this role include: LEVEL 5 –Scientist I Conduct research, development, and production of RNA and related products under the Institute’s Quality Management System(s) within agreed timelines as required. Assist in the design and execution of research and development activities to improve processes and methods for the production of RNA and related products. Liaise with internal and external stakeholders, researchers and collaborators, and actively participate in meetings and discussions as required. Assist custodians for specialised instruments within your area of expertise supporting maintenance, operation, troubleshooting and training of new users. Monitor the effectiveness of existing procedures and provide advice on research and development activities to improve processes and methods. Support implementation, maintenance and continuous improvement of the Institute’s Quality Management Systems (ISO 9001 and/or GMP). Monitor effectiveness of existing processes (scientific, technical and administrative) and advise on proposals to improve operations. Assist with training internal and external stakeholders in the production of RNA and related products as required. Cross-train in related methods and perform quality control analysis of RNA and related products as required. Proactively assist with research reports, peer-reviewed publications, presentations, ethics applications, technical procedures, method documentation, safe work procedures and other published materials. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. LEVEL 6 –Scientist II (In addition to the above) Design and execute research and development activities to improve processes and methods for the production of RNA and related products. Liaise with internal and external stakeholders, researchers and collaborators, and actively lead meetings and discussions as required. Manage smaller research/industry-based projects or elements of larger projects within and/or across the Institute, leading staff and influencing other internal and external partners to achieve the project's goals. Act as custodian for specialised instruments within your area of expertise supporting procurement, installation, and servicing, while managing maintenance, operation, troubleshooting and training of new users. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) LEVEL 5 –Scientist I To be successful in this role you will have: A degree in Molecular Biology, Biochemistry or related discipline, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through a combination of education, training or experience. Demonstrated knowledge in pDNA and mRNA production techniques, in particular downstream processing such as HPLC, FPLC and TFF. Demonstrated knowledge in RNA analytical techniques. Knowledge and experience working with and troubleshooting a range of technical instruments. Demonstrated knowledge or understanding of Quality Management Systems such as ISO 9001 and/or GMP. Demonstrated high level of attention to detail and diligence in carrying out and documenting complex procedures. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail and the ability to liaise effectively with a range of stakeholders whilst exercising discretion and confidentiality. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. LEVEL 6 –Scientist II (In addition to the above) A degree in Molecular Biology, Biochemistry or related discipline, with subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through a combination of formal education, training or industry experience. Demonstrated experience in techniques used for pDNA and mRNA production, in particular downstream processing such as HPLC, FPLC and TFF. Demonstrated experience in RNA analytical techniques is beneficial. Demonstrated experience managing operation, troubleshooting and training for a range of specialised instruments. Demonstrated experience in Quality Management Systems such as ISO 9001 and/or GMP. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed above in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: UNSW Recruitment E: recruitment@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 8 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW aspires to be the exemplar Australian university and employer of choice for people from diverse backgrounds. UNSW aims to ensure equality in recruitment, development, retention and promotion of staff and that no-one is disadvantaged on the basis of their gender, cultural background, disability, sexual orientation or identity or Indigenous heritage. We encourage everyone who meets the selection criteria to apply. UNSW partners with Australia’s leading diversity organisations, networks, and campaigns. Please refer to UNSW’s diversity offerings for further information on our flexible work and leave options, and support for carers (childcare, parent rooms, parental leave). Position Description Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 08 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/541941/research-infrastructure-strategist-digital": {
      "title": "Research Infrastructure Strategist (Digital)",
      "description": "Research Infrastructure Strategist (Digital) Apply now Job no: 541941 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Project Management, Research Infrastructure Employment Type: Full time Remuneration: $153,933 - $163,338 + 17% super Duration: 2-year term contract Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) Why Your Role Matters: NSW’s digital research infrastructure provides the platforms, services and capabilities that enable researchers to undertake innovative, high-impact research. The Project Manager plays a strategic role within Research Technology Services (ResTech), leading the establishment, governance and implementation of shared digital research capabilities that support UNSW’s long-term research priorities. You will lead significant university-wide initiatives, including the establishment of AI Central as a research capability and UNSW’s partnership with the Australian Urban Research Infrastructure Network (AURIN). Working with senior leaders and stakeholders across UNSW and the national research infrastructure sector, you will shape governance arrangements, operating models, investment priorities and delivery approaches. The position reports to the Manager, Research Data and Software and works closely with the Executive Director, Research Technology Services and the Pro-Vice-Chancellor, Research Infrastructure. About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We are a large organisation with a diverse and highly talented community doing extraordinary work. We are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in all we do, and this collective approach is what gives our work impact. It is also why UNSW is consistently ranked among the world’s top 20 universities in the QS World University Rankings and is a proud member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re seeking a career where you can thrive, be challenged and contribute to meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible and hybrid working arrangements. 17% employer superannuation contributions. Additional leave over the end-of-year university shutdown period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Affordable on-campus parking You'll bring: You will have a relevant tertiary qualification and or experience, together with: Advanced knowledge of shared digital research infrastructure and service capabilities, including an understanding of current NCRIS programs and priorities. Extensive experience leading complex, strategic projects through the full project lifecycle. Demonstrated experience developing business cases, project plans, governance frameworks, operating models and high-quality project documentation. Excellent consultation, influencing and negotiation skills, with the ability to build alignment among diverse and senior stakeholders. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to develop practical and sustainable solutions to complex issues. Experience managing project budgets, resources, risks, dependencies and competing priorities. Advanced written and verbal communication skills with strong attention to detail. A collaborative, proactive and adaptable approach, with the judgement and initiative required to operate effectively in a complex environment. How to Apply: Please submit your CV and Cover Letter via the Apply button before 11.55pm, Thursday 24 th September 2026. As part of the application process, you will also be asked to respond to two short free-format questions designed to help us better understand your experience. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Shiree Thomas, Talent Acquisition Partner e: shiree.thomas@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 24 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542197/senior-research-fellow-social-science-specialist": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow - Social Science Specialist",
      "description": "Senior Research Fellow - Social Science Specialist Apply now Job no: 542197 Work type: Full Time Location: Other location Categories: Senior Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Biological, Earth and Environmental Sciences is a large, collaborative and multi-disciplinary school in the Faculty of Science at the University of New South Wales with over 170 academic, research and professional staff. In 2025, the school obtained $22M in research funding, produced over 800 scientific publications and is frequently rated among the top schools across UNSW in student experience surveys. The School incorporates a broad range of disciplines, enabling comprehensive education and research in the broad fields of Earth Science, Environmental Science, Physical Geography, Climate Science, Evolutionary Biology, Ecology and Marine Science and Oceanography. The Senior Research Fellow – Social Science Specialist in the Centre for Sustainable Development Reform (CSDR) is expected to lead, with Director oversight, the research effort of the CSDR through identifying opportunities to integrate social and equity considerations across the research portfolio, while developing their research expertise through the pursuit of defined projects relevant to their particular field. The CSDR team is made up of multi-disciplinary focussed researchers who are working on topics at the intersections of economics, politics, law, environmental management, data science and development. The Senior Research Fellow leads the development of robust social accounting frameworks and works to understand how bespoke surveys, standardised data sets, advanced quantitative analysis, and qualitative data can inform government policy and decision making in the marine environment, while building capacity among national statistical offices and government agencies to implement these frameworks for developing an equitable and sustainable ocean economy. This role also works with coastal communities and governments to shape development planning through the application of frameworks such as social accounts which document livelihood dependencies, stewardship contributions, and traditional knowledge and make visible the importance of nature to people. About the role Level C ($155k - $178k) plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 2.5 years Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Lead social science expertise as a senior team member and project leader in collaborative interdisciplinary research concerning topics: sustainable development of the ocean economy and ocean-based sectors, social accounting, sustainable development policy and practise partnerships with Pacific Islands countries and beyond, design of legislative and policy enabling frameworks for Beyond GDP development, sustainable development of natural capital and ecosystem services. Provide strategic leadership for the social accounting research program within CSDR, including setting methodological standards for the Global Ocean Accounts Partnership. Lead the design, testing, and refinement of social accounting methodologies for ocean accounts, including the development of indicators, data collection protocols, and analytical frameworks that can be adapted across diverse cultural and institutional contexts. Collaborate with colleagues across all relevant projects within the CSDR to support the broad spectrum of work being undertaken. This role will focus on the integration of social and equity considerations across the breadth of the research portfolio of the CSDR. Disseminate findings to create scholarly impact which is globally and locally recognised by peers through academic journal articles, conferences and presentations, and publicly accessible knowledge products that advance the utilisation of disciplinary knowledge. Represent the CSDR as a recognised expert in social accounting for ocean economies at national and international forums, serving on expert panels, advisory committees, and editorial boards. Make independent contributions to research that have a significant impact in their field of expertise and create a nationally recognised research track record. Undertake independent research in discipline or related area (as per the norms of the discipline), making an independent contribution through professional practice and expertise. Develop research groups in area of specialist expertise and research methods that are appropriate to the discipline, including developing independent research proposals which can include the growth of a team of social scientists working across the three pillars of the CSDR’s work. Responsible for a small group of researchers, with the ability to motivate and deliver through the team and be accountable for their performance. Contribute to the general growth of the CSDR through the development of government and donor relations; fundraising, grant application preparation and competitive funding applications under the guidance of senior colleagues. This will include participating as co-investigator or chief investigator in grant applications. Mentor and guide students, groups, and colleagues; supervise HDRs to timely completion as primary supervisor; and contribute to HDR review panels. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Ensure hazards and risks are identified and controlled for tasks, projects and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience in policy advisory positions including for government, consultancies, or in applied research. Demonstratable experience in one or more of the following areas; Societal and equity considerations as they relate to marine and coastal issues Design and delivery of bespoke surveys, preferably using survey software Social aspects of natural capital accounting and/or natural resource use Framework development experience in social accounting, natural capital accounting, or related systematic approaches to measuring societal outcomes Understanding of how to assess cultural and social capital Practical experience in participatory and co-design approaches for natural resource management Integration of qualitative local knowledge into traditional scientific systems The delivery of national surveys such as the Household Income and Expenditure Surveys Advanced quantitative analytical capabilities potentially including statistical modelling, econometric analysis, database design and management, and proficiency with analytical software (R, Python, STATA, or similar). Demonstrated track record in research and applied project leadership with outcomes of high quality and high impact, including evidence of successful delivery of complex, multi-stakeholder initiatives. Ability to independently lead a significant area of work with multiple stakeholders and partners. Demonstrated ability to lead teams and work in a team, collaborate across disciplines, manage complex project workflows. Established reputation with evidence of thought leadership in relevant areas, including keynote presentations and advisory roles with government agencies or international organizations and proven ability to also write for a policy audience. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. High level communication skills and ability to network effectively and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines, create innovative solutions to complex problems and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. Experience working in varied cultural contexts, interacting with government ministers and senior officials, and experience in training and workshop delivery are all highly desirable. Demonstrated experience in working in developing countries, preferably with coastal communities and/or natural resource management is highly desirable Evidence of an ability to raise funds from a range of sources to support their work and a wider team. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Juliet Grimm E: juliet.grimm@unsw.edu.au Applications close: September 22nd, 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW aspires to be the exemplar Australian university and employer of choice for people from diverse backgrounds. UNSW aims to ensure equality in recruitment, development, retention and promotion of staff and that no-one is disadvantaged on the basis of their gender, cultural background, disability, sexual orientation or identity or Indigenous heritage. We encourage everyone who meets the selection criteria to apply. UNSW partners with Australia’s leading diversity organisations, networks, and campaigns. Please refer to UNSW’s diversity offerings for further information on our flexible work and leave options, and support for carers (childcare, parent rooms, parental leave). Position Description Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 22 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542744/senior-education-teaching-support-manager-faculty-of-medicine-health": {
      "title": "Senior Education & Teaching Support Manager- Faculty of Medicine & Health",
      "description": "Senior Education & Teaching Support Manager- Faculty of Medicine & Health Apply now Job no: 542744 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Strategy, Management Employment Type: Full Time; 35 hours per week Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Competitive Remuneration Package – Senior Appointment Location: Kensington Campus, New South Wales . Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Senior Education & Teaching Support Manager is a senior strategic leadership position within UNSW Medicine & Health, providing Faculty-wide leadership and operational oversight of education, teaching and student experience support during a significant period of educational transformation. As a member of the Faculty’s senior professional leadership team, the role leads the development and implementation of a future-focused operating model, bringing together teaching support, education and student support, and education development functions to deliver high-quality, consistent and sustainable services across schools, programs and campus-based environments. A key focus of the position is transformation and change leadership, service integration and operational excellence. Working closely with senior academic and professional leaders, the role will redesign service delivery models and workflows, strengthen governance and consistency, optimise workforce deployment, and embed technology, automation, AI-enabled tools and data to support scalable and contemporary ways of working. The position will provide strategic leadership and oversight across a significant portfolio of transformative initiatives, including: Faculty-wide curriculum transformation and implementation of program-level assessment Transition to a Flex-Semester calendar Medicine Program redesign Replacement of the eMed platform and implementation of the Risr ePortfolio Implementation of the University’s future Learning Management System Expansion of clinical placements and work integrated learning across Health Sciences programs Growth in hospital-based and simulation-based learning activities across multiple health disciplines Establishment of a graduate-entry Nursing Program The role also provides significant workforce leadership, leading and developing a high-performing management team and building the capability, structures and workforce plans required to support future education models and changing service requirements. The Senior Education & Teaching Support Manager provides strategic advice to Faculty leadership on education operations, workforce planning, organisational capability and service delivery, while overseeing integrated Faculty-wide support across teaching operations, assessment, curriculum administration, academic quality, accreditation, student experience and work integrated learning. Through strong partnerships across the Faculty, University and external clinical and education networks, the role will foster a culture of collaboration, innovation, accountability and continuous improvement while ensuring education services remain responsive, efficient and focused on outstanding educational and student outcomes. Some key skills required: Relevant tertiary qualification and extensive senior leadership experience in higher education or a comparable complex environment. Demonstrated success leading large operational portfolios and multidisciplinary teams. Significant experience leading organisational transformation, service redesign and change management initiatives. Proven experience developing and implementing strategic plans and operating models. Demonstrated ability to lead large-scale operational change associated with technology implementation and business process transformation. Extensive experience managing complex stakeholder environments and influencing senior leaders. Strong understanding of higher education teaching, curriculum, student administration and quality assurance frameworks. Demonstrated capability in workforce planning, resource management and organisational development. Desirable Experience leading educational transformation programs within a university environment. Experience implementing major enterprise technology platforms supporting teaching and learning. Understanding of clinical education, placements and health professional accreditation requirements. Experience using technology, data analytics and AI-enabled solutions to improve service delivery and operational efficiency Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application including your resume & cover letter addressing the selection criteria, online before Tuesday 29 th September at 11:55pm. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, WWCC and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 29 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542798/senior-project-officer-research-strategy-office-multiple-roles": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer – Research Strategy Office (multiple roles)",
      "description": "Senior Project Officer – Research Strategy Office (multiple roles) Apply now Job no: 542798 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Administration support, Project Management Employment Type: 3 x full time, continuing (permanent) positions Remuneration: Level 7 ($118,483 - $128,233) + 17% superannuation Working Arrangements: Flexible/Hybrid working The Opportunities Join UNSW’s Research Strategy Office as a Senior Project Officer and contribute to the delivery of strategic research programs and initiatives across the University. Working across the Research Programs, Researcher Pathways and Collaborative Research Development portfolios, you will coordinate projects, develop and monitor project plans, track deliverables, manage risks and issues, prepare high-quality reporting and communications, and support funding and partnership opportunities. You’ll also build effective relationships with Faculties, Strategic Initiatives, Innovation & Industry, researchers and external stakeholders, while helping to ensure projects and funding proposals meet relevant governance and compliance requirements. These roles provide an opportunity for organised, collaborative and proactive project professionals to contribute to UNSW’s research priorities in a complex, purpose-driven environment. Available Positions Senior Project Officer – Research Programs (Ref 52798) x 1 Provide dedicated project management, reporting, analytical and communications support across Research Programs portfolios, Faculties, Strategic Initiatives, Innovation & Industry and external stakeholders and coordinate support for management of institutional priorities, partnership opportunities and funding activities. Senior Project Officer – Collaborative Research Development (Ref 542800) x 2 Support the identification, development and coordination of large-scale, multidisciplinary research collaborations and funding opportunities. Working with Faculties, Strategic Initiatives, Innovation & Industry and external partners, you will coordinate complex activity, build effective partnerships and help translate emerging opportunities into practical, institutionally aligned outcomes. About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We are a large organisation with a diverse and highly talented community doing extraordinary work. We are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in all we do, and this collective approach is what gives our work impact. It is also why UNSW is consistently ranked among the world’s top 20 universities in the QS World University Rankings and is a proud member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re seeking a career where you can thrive, be challenged and contribute to meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Hybrid and flexible working arrangements that support work-life balance while delivering meaningful outcomes 17% superannuation contributions, annual leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice option Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Affordable on-campus parking About you You have a relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent experience in research, higher education or government, together with: Demonstrated experience coordinating projects, programs or initiatives across a complex organisation. Experience developing project plans, objectives and supporting documentation, and monitoring deliverables across the project lifecycle. The ability to identify, monitor and help resolve project risks and issues. Experience supporting stakeholder engagement and building effective relationships across multiple organisational units and with external stakeholders. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with strong attention to detail in reports, communications and other deliverables. Strong organisational and time management skills, including the ability to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and respond effectively to changing requirements. Demonstrated judgement, initiative, analytical and problem-solving skills. The ability to work independently while contributing positively and collaboratively as part of a team. Proficiency in Microsoft Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Copilot. An understanding of the research funding environment and major funding schemes is desirable. Commitment to UNSW’s values, policies, governance requirements and health and safety responsibilities. How to apply: Please submit your CV and a cover letter outlining your interest in the role and how your skills and experience align with the position requirements by 11.55pm on Sunday 27 September 2026. As part of your application, you will also be asked to respond to two application questions. The position descriptions are available on the JOBS@UNSW website. Pre Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Shiree Thomas – Talent Acquisition Consultant e: shiree.thomas@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contact person will not be accepted UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-ST1 Position Description Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542837/associate-lecturer-lecturer-pharmacology-school-of-biomedical-sciences": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer (Pharmacology)- School of Biomedical Sciences",
      "description": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer (Pharmacology)- School of Biomedical Sciences Apply now Job no: 542837 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Lecturer, Associate Lecturer Employment Type: Full Time; 35 hours per week Duration: Fixed Term Until 31 December 2027 (Leave Replacement) Remuneration: Academic Level A/B ($118K - $142K) based on experience + 17% super+ annual leave loading Location: Kensington Campus, New South Wales . Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Associate Lecturer / Lecturer (Education Focussed) contributes to educational excellence in Pharmacology through the delivery of high-quality, engaging teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. This is a hands-on teaching role, and the successful candidate will bring practical experience in pharmacology teaching, alongside a strong commitment to student learning and educational excellence. At Level A (Associate Lecturer) , the role delivers classes, assesses student learning and supports the development of learning activities, resources and assessments with guidance from senior academics. The position also supports student progression and contributes to course administration, academic quality assurance and innovative approaches to teaching and learning. At Level B (Lecturer) , the role operates with greater independence in the design and delivery of teaching, assessment and learning activities. The position manages course administration as Course Authority, contributes to the development of innovative teaching approaches and plays an active role in strengthening teaching quality and the student learning experience across the School. Some key skills required: At Level A: A PhD in a Pharmacology and/or related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Actively stays up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated experience in and passion for teaching and learning design, development and/or delivery at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level. Knowledge of educational technologies and online delivery methods. A strong commitment to work-integrated learning (desirable). Experience engaging students and fostering independent learning through meaningful assessment and helpful feedback. Evidence of and/or preparedness to undertake professional development of teaching practice. At Level B (in addition to the above): Evidence of teaching effectiveness and passion for educational excellence (e.g. relevant discipline-based curriculum design and development at a variety of levels and scales) Demonstrated knowledge of industry or experience of work-integrated learning (desirable) Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application including your resume & cover letter addressing the selection criteria, online before Tuesday 22 nd September at 11:55pm. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, WWCC and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 22 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542949/contracts-manager-research-grants-contracts": {
      "title": "Contracts Manager - Research Grants & Contracts",
      "description": "Contracts Manager - Research Grants & Contracts Apply now Job no: 542949 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Administration support, Legal, Governance, Policy & Compliance Full-time (35 hours per week), Continuing (permanent) role Flexible hybrid working and optional 9-day fortnight Salary from $132,445 + 17% superannuation The Opportunity Lead complex, high-impact research grant agreements Strengthen team performance and service delivery Work at the centre of a major research ecosystem Research Grants and Contracts (RGC) within the Division of Research & Enterprise contributes to UNSW’s research performance and profile via the effective management of the University’s research income. RGC is the key link between UNSW researchers, executive and funding agencies such as the NHMRC, ARC, other government and non- government research funders, philanthropic competitive grant funders and industry partners. Each year the RGC manages more than 2800 applications and 6000 ongoing projects, supporting more than 2,000 UNSW academics. The Contracts Manager is a senior member of the team, responsible for delivering high-quality, customer-focused services relating to research agreements. The role leads, motivates and supports a team of Contract Officers to achieve team objectives and deliver excellent stakeholder service. Working closely with senior managers and colleagues across the University, the position oversees the review and negotiation of contractual and competitive research agreements, provides expert advice on research contracting matters, and escalates complex issues to legal and Industry & Innovation where appropriate. The role reports to the Senior Manager, Contracts, leads and develops 3+ direct reports and contributes to building a cohesive, capable and effective team. About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We are a large organisation with a diverse and highly talented community doing extraordinary work. We are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in all we do, and this collective approach is what gives our work impact. It is also why UNSW is consistently ranked among the world’s top 20 universities in the QS World University Rankings and is a proud member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re seeking a career where you can thrive, be challenged and contribute to meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to UNSW benefits including: Hybrid and flexible working arrangements (3 days per week onsite at the UNSW Kensington campus) Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period Access to lifelong learning and career development Discounts and entitlements Affordable on-campus parking About you You have relevant qualifications and/or substantial experience in preparing, reviewing and negotiating complex research grant agreements, together with strong contractual, negotiation and stakeholder management skills. You will have demonstrated experience leading, mentoring and developing staff, including setting clear expectations, providing constructive feedback and supporting team members to build their knowledge and confidence. You will be committed to fostering a collaborative, inclusive and customer-focused team culture, while contributing to effective workflow management, quality assurance and continuous improvement. You will also bring sound judgement in managing sensitive and complex matters, and the ability to contribute to strategic planning, policy development and implementation. Strong analytical, prioritisation and time management skills are essential, along with a commitment to UNSW’s values, policies and health and safety responsibilities. To Apply: Please submit your   CV, Cover letter and address selection criteria before 11.55pm on Monday, 21 September 2026, using the Apply button. The position description is available on the JOBS@UNSW website. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact : Shiree Thomas, Talent Acquisition Partner e: shiree.thomas@unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity, diversity, and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 21 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/534991/lecturer-senior-lecturer-school-of-information-systems-technology-management-unsw-business-school": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer - School of Information Systems & Technology Management - UNSW Business School",
      "description": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer - School of Information Systems & Technology Management - UNSW Business School Apply now Job no: 534991 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Senior Lecturer, Lecturer Employment Type: Full time Duration: Tenure Track position Remuneration: Level B from $133,065 + 17% superannuation and leave loading and Level C from $161,619 + 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington campus in NSW Australia About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 50 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The School of Information Systems & Technology Management (SISTM) is one of eight schools within the Business School that comprises over 30 staff from all over the world. It offers a vibrant and dynamic academic community engaged in leading-edge research, innovative teaching as well as constructive community and professional engagement. SISTM is one of the largest and oldest such schools in Australia. The School is currently ranked 1st in Australia, 2nd in Asia, and 15th worldwide in the latest Association of Information Systems (AIS) research productivity ranking and has highly developed local and international links. Why Your Role Matters: The School of Information Systems & Technology Management at UNSW Business School is hiring for two tenure-track positions, at either the level of Lecturer (Level B) or Senior Lecturer (Level C), in the area of Information Systems, Business Analytics, or Operations Management. A Lecturer (Level B) is expected to conduct scholarly research and professional activities, both nationally and internationally, and make substantive contributions in the areas of teaching, engagement and leadership in line with the expectations of the School and Faculty. A Senior Lecturer (Level C) will additionally be expected to conduct high-quality research that is recognised internationally and makes significant contributions to educational leadership and excellence in the School and Faculty. The role reports to the Head of School and has no direct reports. The level of appointment will be commensurate with the candidate’s qualifications and experience. If required, visa sponsorship and relocation support will be considered for the successful candidate. Who You Are: A PhD in Information Systems, Business Analytics, Operations Management, or a closely related business field by the end of 2026. Demonstrated excellence in publishing in leading IS or OM conferences and journals (e.g., UTD24, Financial Times 50, AIS Basket of 8). Experience in teaching technical (AI, database, networking) or business analytics courses is highly desirable, as is experience with UX design. Proven commitment to staying current with discipline knowledge and developments. Experience in teaching and learning design, development and delivery at undergraduate and/or postgraduate levels. Experience with educational technologies, online delivery methods, and industry engagement. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, with a commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. To learn more about this position and view the full list of criteria, a copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Excellent career development opportunities Access to an academic salary supplementation scheme Up to 17% superannuation contributions and leave loading payments Flexible working arrangements (work from home, flexible hours, etc.) Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Complete a 1–2-page cover letter addressing how you meet the requirements of this position and submit your application online by Friday November 6 th at 11:30 pm Sydney Australia time. Get in Touch: There will be select opportunities for informal meeting about the position at INFORMS Annual Meeting in San Francisco. If you are interested in being considered for a meeting, or If you have any questions regarding the recruitment process or role please contact Amy Denney, Talent Acquisition Partner at a.denney@unsw.edu.au . Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role . UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 07 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542020/lecturer-school-of-accounting-auditing-taxation-unsw-business-school": {
      "title": "Lecturer - School of Accounting, Auditing & Taxation - UNSW Business School",
      "description": "Lecturer - School of Accounting, Auditing & Taxation - UNSW Business School Apply now Job no: 542020 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Lecturer Lecturer - School of Accounting, Auditing & Taxation - UNSW Business School Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Convertible Tenure Track Remuneration: Level B from $133,065 - $156,864 + 17% superannuation and annual leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus, NSW About Us: UNSW Business School is a globally recognised leader in business education and research. The School of Accounting, Auditing & Taxation is committed to advancing knowledge and practice in accounting, assurance, and taxation through innovative teaching and impactful research. We maintain strong connections with industry and professional bodies, ensuring our graduates are work-ready and equipped for leadership roles in a rapidly evolving business environment. If required, visa sponsorship and relocation support may be considered for a successful candidate. A competitive salary supplement may be negotiated for suitably qualified candidates. Why This Role Matters We are seeking Lecturers (Level B) (multiple positions) to join the School of Accounting, Auditing & Taxation. We are interested in recruiting candidates with direct and demonstrated research expertise in: Experimental managerial research Archival audit research Empirical tax research Sustainability and climate reporting research These positions are being offered as 5-year fixed term teaching and research convertible tenure track opportunities. If your expertise is in sustainability and climate reporting, the position may be offered as either a balanced Teaching & Research role (5-year contract) or an Education-Focused (EF) role (2-year convertible tenure track contract). The Lecturer position will enable you to build your academic career through high-quality research, excellent teaching and meaningful contribution to the School and Faculty. You will teach across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, undertake impactful research and contribute to the broader academic community. In this role you will: Deliver high-quality teaching and support positive student learning and employability outcomes. Design engaging learning activities, assessments and resources across a range of learning environments. Contribute to curriculum development, course administration and academic quality assurance. Maintain and develop expertise in pedagogy, disciplinary knowledge and relevant professional requirements. Undertake high-quality independent and collaborative research and contribute to the advancement of disciplinary knowledge (balanced teaching and research positions). Develop research projects and contribute to competitive grant applications (balanced teaching and research positions). Contribute to HDR supervision and the development of students and colleagues. Participate in School and Faculty activities, committees, meetings and seminars. Skills and Experience A PhD in a relevant discipline. Demonstrated experience in teaching and learning at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and a commitment to educational excellence. Experience using educational technologies and contemporary delivery methods. A strong research track record, with evidence of high-quality and impactful research (balanced teaching and research positions). The ability to contribute to research leadership, collaboration and competitive funding applications (balanced teaching and research positions). Relevant engagement with industry and/or the profession. A demonstrated ability to support students from diverse backgrounds and contribute to equity, diversity and inclusion. Strong communication, interpersonal and organisational skills. The ability to collaborate effectively, build relationships and work across disciplines. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: Submit your CV and a document outlining how your experience meets the skills and experience criteria (2-4 pages) by 11.55pm Friday 9th October 2026. Get In touch: Role-related enquiries: Professor Noel Harding Head of School, School of Accounting, Auditing & Taxation E: n.harding@unsw.edu.au Recruitment process enquiries: Amy Denney Talent Acquisition Partner E: a.denney@unsw.edu.au Please apply directly through the website , applications sent to the contact above will not be accepted. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role . UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 07 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542416/program-officer-experiential-learning": {
      "title": "Program Officer, Experiential Learning",
      "description": "Program Officer, Experiential Learning Apply now Job no: 542416 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Student Services Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 6 from $108k + 17% superannuation & leave loading Location:  Kensington NSW (Hybrid Working Arrangements) Why your role matters The Program Officer, Experiential Learning plays a key role within the Employability Experiential Learning team in the Pro Vice-Chancellor Student Success portfolio, providing practical and efficient program and course support for the implementation, coordination and continuous improvement of innovative experiential learning initiatives, including work-integrated learning and curriculum skills development courses. The role provides course, project and program support, including reporting, analysis, communications, relationship coordination and administration. Working with internal and external stakeholders, the Program Officer contributes to the effective delivery of experiential learning programs, courses, projects and initiatives, while supporting continuous improvement and positive outcomes for students. The role reports to the Lead, Experiential Learning, and has no direct reports. Specific accountabilities include: Provide program, course, project, operational, and administration support to the Lead, Experiential Learning and the Academic Lead, Experiential Learning. Develop and monitor the delivery of Employability Experiential Learning programs, projects and initiatives to achieve operational and strategic goals in alignment with the UNSW Progress for All strategy. Act as the first point of contact for Experiential Learning enquiries (from students, staff, and external stakeholders), respond to, and escalate, enquiries where appropriate. About You: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, or training and experience. Experience in project development, coordination and delivery of courses and programs, preferably in education, employability and work integrated learning placements in a university environment. Experience in the administration of multi-stakeholder, partnered course delivery, which includes agreements/contracts, risk assessments, work health and safety protocols, and insurance policies. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Demonstrated success in case managing issues to resolution in partnership with subject matter experts across dependency areas of experiential learning including legal, finance, payments, insurance, travel, and other central services, with a strong working knowledge of what matters are covered by each of these central services, and the capacity to identify, refer, and escalate issues to these central services where appropriate and/or necessary. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Experience working with a range of computer systems and applications, ideally including student data and information management systems and career management systems. Proven project experience, with an understanding of the project life cycle and development of project plans, objectives and documentation. Proficiency across Microsoft Office package, particularly in Excel, Forms and Power Automate. Experience with WIL systems such as InPlace and data visualisation software such as Power BI Data integration of students, particularly with reporting Experience with using university Enterprise Systems (SIMS, Term Planning, Timetabling, Enrolments). Understanding of LMS Systems, Moodle, and Canvas For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW . Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Submit your CV & Cover Letter addressing your suitability to the role in relation to the selection criteria (in approximately 2 pages) before 11.55pm Wednesday 16th September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. We strongly encourage you to apply as soon as possible and will be reviewing applications as they are submitted. Interviews will be held on Monday 21st September 2026. Job queries contact: Chloe Gane, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: chloe.gane@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 07 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/541870/rde-program-coordinator": {
      "title": "RDE Program Coordinator",
      "description": "RDE Program Coordinator Apply now Job no: 541870 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: Full time, fixed term role for 27 months as as Program Co-ordinator, RDE program, UNSW IT Starting salary $118483 plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 50 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Program Co-ordinator plays a key role within the Research Data Experience (RDE) Program in providing practical and efficient program support (including proactive administrative support) for the Program and Program Streams. The Program Co-ordinator will ensure that administrative systems and processes are executed to a high standard. The role entails providing program management monitoring and reporting support, coordinating the Program Schedule and the timetables of critical governance groups. The role reports to the RDE Program Manager. Specific accountabilities for this role include: Assist with the development of key program artefacts such as program plans, change requests, functional and non-functional requirements, schedules, and resource plans. This includes working with the project teams including project co-ordinators to ensure strategic alignment of all documents. Support with the leadership and management of the program, driving goal attainment and initiative success through strategic prioritisation and influence. Management, development and coordination of governance documentation and meetings including program diary management, minute taking and management of the program team actions. Manage the seamless integration of project schedules and resource allocation to optimize program execution and guarantee alignment with established timelines. Develop, review, and standardise program-related documents, ensuring consistency in format and content across the program. Develop templates and management systems for common documents to streamline the creation process. Manage the distribution of documents to relevant team members and stakeholders in line with distribution protocols to ensure the right people receive the right information at the right time. Provide program technical advice and guidance, support, and mentorship to team members as well as to external resources on internal processes ensuring adherence to the policies and procedures of the university. Facilitate clear and effective communication within the program team and stakeholders; address issues and concerns promptly, fostering a positive working environment. Coordinate adherence to UNSW governance pathways to ensure the program aligns to the PMO Guardrails Manage, monitor, track, and report on the status of program and deliverables to ensure time, cost and quality metrics are in line with the agreed project outcomes and plans. Liaise with key stakeholders to recommend and determine areas for improvement and develop action plans as a result. Utilise best practice issue and risk management, identify and assist in assessing issues and risks in an informed and considered manner to plan for the mitigation of these, managing the dependencies, and changes in scope with substantial influence over the priorities. Partner with internal and external project colleagues to develop and coordinate change activities including effective communications with key stakeholders, both internal and external to the program. Ensure alignment with the agreed Program/Project Methodology and governance for the related activities and artefacts and ensuring adherence to those standards. Stay informed about changes in governance that may impact program implementation. Support program financial management including the need to track and coordinate vendor Purchase Orders and Invoices in conjunction with Finance. Manage the documentation associated with procurement activities Prepare briefing notes, agendas, minutes, reports, presentations and other documents as required Support with the presentation and set up of shared workspaces, including meeting rooms and print rooms Skills and Experience: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Demonstrated success working effectively and collaboratively on initiatives with a range of people at various levels within an organisation. Experience working with a range of computer systems and applications, including JIRA, Confluence, MS Project, MS Office. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Demonstrated project co-ordination experience monitoring and tracking projects within agreed time limits. Experience managing project finances and knowledge of UNSW Financial policies, procedures and systems. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Proven skills in preparing documentation, presentations and reports, and advanced competency in the use of Microsoft Office, including but not limited to Word, Excel, Outlook and Power Point. Demonstrated ability to exercise initiative and recommend improvements to systems and processes and capacity and willingness to develop knowledge and understanding of advanced administrative processes. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Wednesday 16 th of September at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/541924/site-reliability-engineer-azure": {
      "title": "Site Reliability Engineer (Azure)",
      "description": "Site Reliability Engineer (Azure) Apply now Job no: 541924 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Full-time continuing role within UNSW IT as a Site Reliability Engineer ( Azure ) Starting Salary $132,445 plus generous superannuation and leave loading Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Site Reliability Engineer (Azure) is a specialist role within the Integration Technologies team, responsible for ensuring the reliability, availability, performance, and operational excellence of Azure-based integration platforms and services. This role focuses on the production and non-production environments supporting enterprise integrations (e.g., Azure Data Factory, Logic Apps, API Management, Event Hub), applying SRE principles to proactively manage system health, automate operations, reduce operational toil, and continuously improve service resilience and observability. The role bridges engineering and operations, working closely with integration developers, platform teams, and vendors to ensure solutions are supportable, scalable, and aligned with operational acceptance standards. Specific accountabilities for this role include: Own the reliability and availability of Azure integration services across production and non production environments Monitor system health, performance, and availability using Azure Monitor, Log Analytics, Application Insights, DataDog and other observability tooling Define, implement, and maintain Service Level Objectives and other metrics for key services Lead incident response, triage, root cause analysis, and post-incident reviews (PIRs) Reduce operational toil through automation using scripting (PowerShell, Azure CLI), IaC (Bicep/Terraform), and pipelines Implement self-healing mechanisms, alerting improvements, and proactive remediation patterns o Maintain and improve CI/CD pipelines for operational deployments and configuration changes Manage and maintain Azure integration platform components including API Management, Logic Apps, Function Apps, Data Factory, SQL, Databrick, Service Bus/Event Hub o Ensure environments (dev/test/prod) are consistent, secure, and supportable Oversee configuration management including Key Vault integration, secrets rotation, certificates, and connectivity (e.g., private endpoints, hybrid connections) o Enhance logging, monitoring, and tracing (e.g., distributed tracing with tools like App Insights) Analyse trends in incidents, performance, and usage to identify systemic improvements Maintain operational dashboards and reporting for service health and performance Contribute to and enforce Operational Acceptance Criteria (OAC) for new and changed integration solutions Be available for On-Call / After Hours support on a rotating basis, typically one week per month as well as weekend and After Hours work as required Ensure solutions meet standards for supportability, monitoring, alerting, and documentation before production release Maintain runbooks, support documentation, and knowledge base articles o Work closely with Integration Engineers, Data Engineers, and Architects to embed reliability into solution design Partner with security and platform teams on compliance, patching, and platform upgrades Engage with vendors and third-parties for issue resolution and service improvements Ensure adherence to security, governance, and compliance requirements (e.g., identity, access, secrets management) Support audit activities and implement remediation actions where required Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values o Ensure hazards and risks psychosocial and physical are identified and controlled for tasks, projects, and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. Who you are: Essential - Strong hands-on experience with Microsoft Azure, particularly integration services (ADF, Logic Apps, Function Apps, API Management, Event Hub/Service Bus, SQL, Databricks) - Experience applying Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) principles in cloud environments - Proficiency in monitoring and observability tools (Azure Monitor, Log Analytics, Application Insights) - Experience with automation and scripting (PowerShell, Azure CLI) and Infrastructure as Code (Bicep, Terraform) - Strong incident management and problem-solving skills, including root cause analyse - Experience managing production systems with high availability and reliability requirements. Desirable - Experience with distributed tracing and advanced observability (e.g., OpenTelemetry, Jaeger, AppInsights) - Knowledge of CI/CD pipelines (Azure DevOps, GitHub Actions) - Familiarity with integration patterns and enterprise integration architecture - Experience working in hybrid cloud or complex enterprise environments - Understanding of FinOps and cost optimisation in Azure. Behavioural Capabilities - Strong analytical and systems-thinking mindset  - Proactive and continuous improvement focus o Ability to balance operational stability with delivery demands - Effective communication and stakeholder engagement skills - An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines - Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Thursday 17th of September at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542203/integration-analyst": {
      "title": "Integration Analyst",
      "description": "Integration Analyst Apply now Job no: 542203 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology F ull-time continuing role within UNSW IT as an Integration Engineer Starting Salary $118,483 plus generous superannuation and leave loading Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Integration Analyst will play a key role within Corporate Services Integration domain in IT. The Integration Analyst's role is to plan, coordinate, and supervise activities related to the integration of applications into UNSW information systems. This individual is responsible for planning and coordinating the processes required for the provision of interfaces and data exchange/distribution necessary for UNSW operations. This position reports to Integration Technology Lead and has no direct reports. Specific accountabilities for this role include: Plan, execute, and manage the integration of new applications into existing network infrastructure, systems, and software throughout the enterprise. Develop custom middleware to assist in the transformation and control of data between systems. Manage deployments to reduce risk and provide production support where required. Where applicable, investigate and qualify potential areas in which to introduce Web API or integration services. Be available for On-Call / After Hours support on a rotating basis, typically one week per month as well as weekend and After Hours work as required. Collaborate with technology team members, end users, and other stakeholders in the testing of new Interfaces and applications. Ensure that any new integration solution meets functional requirements, system compliance, and other specifications. Provide technical support, triage support issues, troubleshoot, identify root cause, and coordinate and escalate to key internal and external resources as required, to effectively resolve all issues. Monitor and identify patterns of issues, identify potential points of failure, and recommend permanent solutions. Application and integration testing, assist UAT testing and prepare release notes for deployment. Maintain integration catalogue and update system documentation. Proactively identify complex risks and issues, work with the team to ensure appropriate solutions are in place. Provide functional level 2 and 3 support to system and process owners on business processes, implement functionality, and business system capabilities. Support leadership in optimising the way in which the integration sub domain work, including practices, organisation, and resourcing to promote team dynamics and opportunities for continuous improvement. Be actively involved in communications with key stakeholders, both internal and external and provide influential input with stakeholders to achieve business outcomes. Develop operational and user guides and develop and deliver end-user training. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. Who you are: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training, and experience. 2-3 years of experience working on Middleware platforms i.e. Microsoft Azure platform and native integrations. Experience with Microsoft Azure Data factory is desirable but not essential. Demonstrated experience in requirements gathering, use cases documentation, gap analysis, systems design, and integration. Knowledge of and experience in a range of application development methodologies including ‘agile’, ‘waterfall’, ‘lean’ and ‘rapid’ application development methodologies. Strong knowledge and competency in the use of professional tools i.e. JIRA, confluence, Testing tools (Selenium) etc. Well-developed skills in the use of standard office toolsets including word processors, spreadsheets, drawing/diagramming tools (e.g., Visio), presentation tools (e.g., PowerPoint) and project management tools (e.g., MS Project). Sound consultation, influencing and negotiation skills and proven ability to engage effectively with diverse stakeholders to achieve successful outcomes. Excellent analytical and problem-solving skills and proven capacity to exercise initiative, flexibility and to be proactive in development of robust solutions to problems. Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Wednesday 23rd of September at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 23 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
