    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542608/project-manager-it-focused": {
      "title": "Project Manager – IT Focused",
      "description": "Project Manager – IT Focused Apply now Job no: 542608 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management Employment Type: Full Time, 35 hours per week, Fixed Term 2-year contract. Remuneration: Level 8 - from $132,445 plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington with transition to newly renovated office at our Randwick campus, anticipated for October 2026 About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. About the Role The Project Manager plays a key leadership role within the Business Optimisation Strategy (BOS) Program, leading assigned technology and operational workstreams across planning, development, and implementation. The position ensures delivery aligns with Program priorities, IT governance standards, enterprise architecture, and strategic outcomes. Working closely with cross-functional IT delivery teams, vendors, and business stakeholders, the role translates strategic goals into structured technical delivery plans. It applies specialist project management expertise to navigate dependencies across cloud platforms (e.g., Azure), systems integration, software delivery lifecycles, and modern application ecosystems. This role reports to the Senior PMO Lead and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Lead the end-to-end planning and implementation of assigned IT and technology workstreams, collaborating with enterprise architects, software engineers, vendors, and business SMEs to ensure aligned execution. Define workstream scope, technical deliverables, and milestones, maintaining detailed delivery plans, resource forecasts, and budget oversight across complex software and systems initiatives. Oversee technology vendors, system integrators, and software suppliers, managing contractual deliverables, performance SLAs, expenditure, and technical dependencies. Identify and mitigate technical, delivery, physical, and psychosocial risks early, ensuring alignment with UNSW IT governance, cybersecurity principles, and project controls. Facilitate engagement between technical delivery teams, business operations, and academic stakeholders to support smooth systems transition, testing, quality assurance, and benefits realization. Provide high-quality technical advice, status reporting, and risk insights to project leadership, supporting effective decision-making across the software development and deployment lifecycle. About You Relevant tertiary qualifications with a focus on Project Management in IT, Computer Science, or a related discipline, with substantial professional experience leading complex technology and software delivery initiatives. Proven track record (5+ years) managing IT projects across the full lifecycle within large, matrixed enterprise environments, with exposure to cloud architectures (e.g., Microsoft Azure) and systems integration landscapes. Strong familiarity with modern software development lifecycles, API ecosystems, testing frameworks, and IT governance protocols. Exceptional vendor management and commercial acumen, with practical experience managing technical suppliers, project budgets, and resource allocation using project systems and tools. Advanced stakeholder management and communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to bridge the gap between technical engineering teams and non-technical business leaders. Outstanding time management and initiative, with a strong commitment to UNSW values, governance policies, and physical and psychosocial safety standards. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before 11:55pm, Sunday 20 September 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 20 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542674/postdoctoral-fellow": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Post-doctoral Fellow Apply now Job no: 542674 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate, Senior Research Associate Employment Type: Part-time (14 hours per week) Duration: 12-month fixed-term contract Remuneration: Level A: $95,036 - $126,711 + 17% Super + Leave Loading (pro rata) Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Post-Doctoral Fellow will undertake the dedicated research project, ‘Oral Her-story Archive: Feminist Activism in Response to Child Sexual Assault in New South Wales (1980-present)’. The project will build an oral history archive preserving a significant yet under-documented history of feminist activism and children’s rights advocacy in response to child sexual assault in NSW, from grassroots campaigns of the 1980s through to the present. The archive will capture first-hand accounts from key activists, advocates, government agency staff, sector workers and other stakeholders, while documenting the broader social, political and legal contexts that shaped the period. The archive will form the foundation for a comprehensive history of child sexual assault in Australia from the 1980s onwards. The history has been generously funded by Gillian Calvert AO, a leading figure in the feminist movement against child sexual abuse (CSA) and NSW's inaugural Commissioner for Children and Young People (1999-2009), and Clare Donnellan, a former women’s health worker, midwife and Senior Executive in statutory child protection. The position reports to the historian and gender studies scholar, Associate Professor Zora Simic, and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Liaise with key stakeholders and interview participants. Develop interview protocols and oral history methodology. Manage UNSW ethics processes and compliance requirements. Conduct interviews, coordinate transcripts and code interviews. Organise materials for archive cataloguing and digitising in advance of a public-facing website. Liaise to access personal archives and supplementary materials. Work with relevant repositories to prepare for storage of materials. Undertake preliminary literature review and contextual historical research. Prepare funding applications. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: A PhD (or equivalent experience) in History or another relevant field, such as gender/cultural studies or sociology. Experience in oral history and/or qualitative interviewing, particularly on sensitive topics. Demonstrable awareness of trauma-informed and feminist methodologies. A record of research and/or work experience relevant to the themes of the project. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV, cover letter and a separate document addressing your suitability for the role against the selection criteria listed under ‘Skills & Experience’ in the Position Description via the application portal. Applications close: 11:30pm on the 27th of September. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542701/associate-lecturerlecturer-exercise-physiology": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer/Lecturer - Exercise Physiology",
      "description": "Associate Lecturer/Lecturer - Exercise Physiology Apply now Job no: 542701 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Lecturer, Associate Lecturer Employment Type: Full Time; 35 hours per week Duration: Fixed Term Until 31 December 2030. This position is due to commence in January 2027. Remuneration: Academic Level A/B ($118K - $142K) based on experience + 17% super+ annual leave loading Location: Kensington Campus, New South Wales . Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Associate Lecturer / Lecturer (Education Focussed) contributes to educational excellence in Exercise Science and Exercise Physiology , with a particular focus on neurological rehabilitation. The role supports the development and delivery of high-quality teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, creating engaging and inclusive learning experiences that promote student achievement, progression and positive employability outcomes. At Level A (Associate Lecturer) , the role delivers teaching, supports student learning and contributes to the development of learning activities, resources, assessments and course administration with guidance from senior academics. At Level B (Lecturer) , the role operates with greater independence, designing and developing innovative teaching approaches, managing courses as Course Authority and contributing to academic quality assurance. Across both levels, the position contributes to program accreditation activities, maintains contemporary expertise in pedagogy and exercise physiology, and actively participates in the broader School and Faculty academic community. Some key skills required: At Level A: Accreditation with Exercise and Sports Science Australia as an exercise physiologist (highly desirable) Demonstrated knowledge and experience in exercise physiology practice, particularly neurological rehabilitation e.g. spinal cord injury Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills Evidence of ability to support and inspire people from diverse backgrounds and support equity diversity and inclusion initiatives An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training At Level B (in addition to the above): A PhD in exercise physiology, or related discipline, and/or relevant work experience Demonstrated experience in learning and teaching/delivery of courses at an undergraduate and/or postgraduate level, with evidence of teaching effectiveness Experience developing and/or implementing educational technologies and online delivery methods. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application including your resume & cover letter addressing the selection criteria, online before Sunday 4 th October 2026 at 11:55pm. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, WWCC and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 04 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542852/lms-platform-engineer-hybrid": {
      "title": "LMS Platform Engineer (Hybrid)",
      "description": "LMS Platform Engineer (Hybrid) Apply now Job no: 542852 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Engineer Employment Type : Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed-term contract (2 years) until 30th June 2028 Remuneration: $132,445 - $149,228 + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus Hybrid Work Arrangements: Must be available to come to the office 2-3 times a week About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: As an LMS Engineer, you’ll play a critical role in shaping how learning is delivered and experienced across the University. Sitting at the intersection of technology and education, you will design, build, and enhance learning platforms that enable seamless, engaging, and scalable digital learning experiences for staff and students. In this role, your expertise will ensure that LMS solutions are not only technically robust but also aligned to the evolving needs of the University. By leveraging both out-of-the-box capabilities and developing tailored solutions across cloud and on-premise environments, you’ll help deliver flexible, integrated systems that connect with a broader technology ecosystem and support innovation in teaching and learning. Working closely with developers and stakeholders, you’ll guide best-practice solution development using agile and iterative methodologies, driving continuous improvement and high-quality outcomes. Your ability to bridge technical and functional domains will ensure that platforms are reliable, secure, and fit-for-purpose. Ultimately, your work will directly contribute to enhancing UNSW’s digital learning environment—ensuring that systems are efficient, scalable, and capable of supporting world-class education now and into the future. Purpose of the role: Deliver the technical build, configuration, and integration work required to stand up UNSW’s new Learning Management System (LMS) and support the platform and ecosystem commitments through to go-live, noting the new LMS application has not yet been announced. Key responsibilities 1. Platform configuration and build ·         Configure LMS toolsets, features, org structure (faculties, courses, communities), home pages, widgets, and dashboards in line with UNSW requirements . ·         Support role and permissions configuration (scoped roles at system, faculty, course, and assessment level) ·         Configure institutional branding, course templates, and blueprint courses 2. Integrations ·         Assist with the development of integrations between LMS and UNSW systems of record (PeopleSoft/SIS, Entra ID/SSO, Turnitin, Inspera, Leganto, STACK, and other LTI tools) using the LMS vendor Standard CSV, APIs, and LTI Advantage standards ·         Work with the integration ·         Support SSO/identity configuration work delivered through the LMS vendor Professional Services during implementation 3. Data, reporting and analytics ·         Assist with support Data Hub exports, Insights Dashboards, and course-level dashboards for institutional reporting ·         Coordinate with the Data Engineer roles on data flows into UNSW's reporting/BI environment 4. Testing and quality ·         Support configuration testing against acceptance criteria developed with UNSW stakeholders ·         Work with the Test teams on test planning and defect resolution 5. Migration support ·         Support course conversion/migration activity from the incumbent LMS, working with the new LMS’ bulk migration tooling and UNSW's export packages ·         Work with learning designers to remediate migration issues 6. Business case and program delivery support ·         Support stage-gate and governance reporting to the LMS Steering Committee, ITPMO, and Student Systems Reference Group as required For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant degree or equivalent experience, with strong expertise across multiple technology domains (e.g. web applications, cloud platforms, infrastructure, databases, security). Demonstrated ability to analyse, design, and implement complex technical solutions, applying new technologies and concepts. Strong experience working in Agile delivery environments, with a focus on iterative and high-quality solution outcomes. Proficiency using delivery and workflow management tools (e.g. JIRA, MS TFS) to track and manage work. Experience preparing and executing test cases, with a solid understanding of end-to-end testing practices. Excellent interpersonal and stakeholder engagement skills, with a proven ability to build strong working relationships across IT and the business. Strong time management and organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. Collaborative and adaptable team player, able to work independently while coaching and supporting others on complex delivery. Strong customer focus, negotiation, and problem-resolution skills, with a commitment to delivering high-quality outcomes. Demonstrated alignment with UNSW values, policies, and health and safety responsibilities. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role via the application portal before 11:30pm on Friday, the 18th of September 2026. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . For queries regarding the position, contact Timothy Hands, the LMS Program Manager, UNSW: timothy.hands@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Ayla Kamal, Talent Acquisition Partner , UNSW: ayla.kamal@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal* Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 18 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542853/faculty-centre-manager": {
      "title": "Faculty Centre Manager",
      "description": "Faculty Centre Manager Apply now Job no: 542853 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Research Administration support, Project Management Employment Type: Full-Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 8: $132,445 - $149,228 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Faculty Centre Manager is responsible for the operational planning, research administration and overall management of three of the Faculty of Arts, Design & Architecture’s Research Centres: City Futures Research Centre, iCinema and the Big Anxiety Research Centre. The role supports effective governance, financial sustainability, research quality, compliance, stakeholder engagement and partnership development across the Centres. Working closely with each Centre’s Director and leadership team, you will support the achievement of research, operational and strategic objectives while fostering a high-performing, customer-focused culture. The role sits within the Research Innovation Portfolio, reports to the Research Innovation Manager and has two direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Provide operational leadership across the Faculty’s Tier 2 Research Centres, fostering a professional and customer-focused culture. Provide high-level advice on business planning, operational priorities and implementation activities. Manage day-to-day Centre operations and oversee governance arrangements, including Advisory Boards, committees and other governance frameworks. Oversee income, expenditure and research budgets, including budget planning, resource allocation and performance monitoring against agreed targets and KPIs. Deliver high-quality research support, including grant development, funding submissions, project budgets and plans, research agreements and contracts, and performance reporting. Ensure compliance with relevant legislative, regulatory, University, Faculty and funding body requirements. Manage complex financial, governance, research performance and workforce reporting across multiple Centres. Oversee Centre engagement and communications strategies to strengthen profile, partnerships and opportunities for research growth and impact. Build and strengthen relationships with research collaborators, industry partners, sponsors and other stakeholders, identifying opportunities to grow partnerships, secure new business and support research sustainability. Identify opportunities for continuous improvement and implement effective solutions. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant tertiary qualifications with subsequent relevant management experience in a research setting or similar, or equivalent competency gained through education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience in complex financial management, reporting and budgeting, with the ability to interpret financial data and provide strategic business advice. Proven experience in operational planning and implementation, including managing resources, people and projects. Demonstrated understanding of complex compliance and governance requirements, with experience establishing frameworks to successfully manage these. High-level organisational skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and competing deadlines in a fast-paced and changing environment. Proven ability to operate autonomously, demonstrate initiative, drive process improvement and resolve complex problems. Excellent consultation, influencing and negotiation skills, with the ability to engage effectively with diverse internal and external stakeholders. Proven ability to negotiate, build and maintain effective research partnerships across government, industry and community sectors. Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with strong interpersonal skills and attention to detail. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your CV and cover letter outlining your suitability for the position in relation to each of the selection criteria (listed under ‘Skills & Experience’ on the PD) via the application portal before 11:30pm on 20 September. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 20 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542942/support-officer-higher-degree-research": {
      "title": "Support Officer - Higher Degree Research",
      "description": "Support Officer - Higher Degree Research Apply now Job no: 542942 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Administration support, Student Recruitment / Admissions The Opportunity Full-time (35 hours week) Fixed-term contact to Sept 2027 Level 6; Salary from $108,021 + 17% super About the Role The Higher Degree Research (HDR) Support Officer plays a key role in providing high level customer service and coordinating a broad range of administration processes to support the University’s HDR community within the Graduate Research School. Key Responsibilities: Deliver high-quality advice and support to HDR applicants, candidates, supervisors and stakeholders across admissions, scholarships, candidature, thesis submission, examination and research training. Act as the primary contact for enquiries, managing correspondence and administrative processes across the HDR lifecycle, including enrolments, deferrals, withdrawals, thesis submissions and scholarship variations. Coordinate social media, peer support and engagement activities that strengthen connection and enhance the HDR candidate experience. Build effective stakeholder relationships, support team workflows and contribute to Graduate Research School initiatives, events and special projects. About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We are a large organisation with a diverse and highly talented community doing extraordinary work. We are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in all we do, and this collective approach is what gives our work impact. It is also why UNSW is consistently ranked among the world’s top 20 universities in the QS World University Rankings and is a proud member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re seeking a career where you can thrive, be challenged and contribute to meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to UNSW benefits including: Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period Access to lifelong learning and career development Progressive HR practices Discounts and entitlements Affordable on-campus parking You will bring Demonstrated experience in customer service, student support or administration within a higher education environment. Highly developed interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to provide clear and accurate advice to diverse stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply policies, procedures and relevant legislation. Excellent organisational and time management skills, with strong attention to detail and the ability to manage competing priorities and deadlines. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively in a team, take initiative and work independently in a fast-paced environment. Experience planning, organising and coordinating projects, events or engagement activities. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and confidence working across digital communication platforms. A commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives, values and relevant policies, including health and safety responsibilities. To Apply: Please submit your CV and respond to the three mandatory questions in the application form before 11.55pm, Thursday 17th September 2026. A copy of the position description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks You may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact : Shiree Thomas, Talent Acquisition Partner e: shiree.thomas@unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity, diversity, and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-ST1 Position Description Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
