    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/539796/principal-adobe-engineer": {
      "title": "Principal Adobe Engineer",
      "description": "Principal Adobe Engineer Apply now Job no: 539796 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Full time continuing role, UNSW IT, Principal Adobe Engineer within UNSW IT. Starting salary $153933 plus 17% super and leave loading Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) 2-3 days in the office About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 50 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Principal Adobe Engineer – Digital Solutions position is responsible for providing enterprise-wide leadership in activation, orchestration and optimisation of customer experiences across UNSW’s digital ecosystem, working primarily on the Adobe Experience Cloud suite and emerging technologies. The role leads how digital, marketing, and customer data platforms are applied to deliver personalised, data driven experiences across channels, enabling content, campaigns, journeys, analytics, and lifecycle engagement to operate as a cohesive capability. The position is accountable for the direction, standards, and governance of customer data, identity, and personalisation platforms, ensuring alignment with UNSW’s digital transformation strategy and business priorities. The Principal Adobe Engineer champions contemporary engineering practices, including Agile delivery and controlled configuration management and environment promotion, and leverages digital platforms to deliver secure, scalable and data‑driven experiences for UNSW. Operating as the University’s technical authority for Adobe and related digital platforms, the role provides strategic leadership and practical guidance across major initiatives, influencing platform usage, journey design, and activation patterns. The Principal Adobe Engineer reports to the Digital Delivery Lead and has no direct reports. Accountabilities: Provide technical design, implementation and optimisation of UNSW’s Adobe Journey Optimizer (AJO) and Adobe Customer Journey Analytics (CJA) on Adobe Experience Platform (AEP) and the Customer Data Platform (CDP), working within agreed enterprise architecture and data governance frameworks and providing expert input into platform roadmaps. Contribute to the definition and practical implementation of enterprise-wide architectural standards and policies for digital platforms, ensuring Adobe Experience Cloud solutions are scalable, secure and aligned with UNSW’s digital transformation strategy. Perform development testing across business processes and platform solutions, defining test scenarios and executing and analysing results. Collaborate with Testing and Analysis colleagues throughout delivery to support outcomes and ensure solutions meet intended business outcomes, while driving quality assurance practices and contributing to enterprise-level assurance frameworks. Write and implement efficient code and thorough documentation, enforcing standards and practices with the digital platforms team in an Agile / Test Driven Development. Assess solution options / approaches and document technical specification. Provide expert technical guidance and thought leadership to cross-functional teams, influencing platform usage, journey design, and activation strategies to achieve business outcomes. Lead technical incident investigation and root cause analysis for Adobe platform-related issues, ensuring lessons learned inform future improvements and risk mitigation strategies in partnership with operations and support teams. Drive continuous improvement of engineering practices, processes and patterns across the University’s Adobe ecosystem, ensuring implementations align with enterprise architectural standards, policies and governance frameworks defined by Architecture, Cyber and Data Governance teams, and acting as a key contributor to the evolution of these standards based on implementation experience. Conduct performance tuning, security assessments, and ensure solutions adhere to university wide cybersecurity policies and threat mitigation strategies. Maintain up to date knowledge of industry trends to identify novel solutions and frameworks and share this knowledge and recommendations with the team to incorporate into digital solutions and platforms. Who you are: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Adobe certification preferred. Extensive engineering / development experience in at least one or more of the Adobe products listed above, with demonstrated leadership of enterprise-scale implementations. Exposure to JavaScript, HMTL, CSS, Python, SQL and RESTful APIs preferred. Hands-on experience with data management, audience segmentation and personalisation strategies. High-level proficiency in working with data layers, tag management systems and tracking implementations, within complex, multi-system environments. Strong understanding of customer data platforms, identity resolution and omnichannel marketing and their application within a large-scale organisation. Extensive experience with cloud technologies, data pipelines and enterprise data governance principles. This includes automated testing / deployment for managing pipeline code and infrastructure. Experience working with Agile and other delivery methodologies and applying best practices, with demonstrated experience leading large cross-functional delivery teams. Developed time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities and take on competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Highly developed stakeholder engagement, consultation, influencing and negotiation skills, with a strong track record of building effective relationships and achieving successful outcomes across diverse groups. Strong analytical, problem-solving and organisational skills, with the ability to exercise initiative and sound judgement, manage changing priorities, balance multiple tasks, and deliver robust solutions within competing deadlines. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Pre employment checks required for this position. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: please apply through the portal, we would like you to submit a full application including resume and addressing the who you are section. Applications close:  Monday 5th of October at 11.30pm Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Jen MacLachlan j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Partner – UNSW IT Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 05 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/539862/lead-integration-engineer-student": {
      "title": "Lead Integration Engineer - Student",
      "description": "Lead Integration Engineer - Student Apply now Job no: 539862 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: full time continuing role, UNSW IT Lead Integration Engineer role within our Student and Research Solutions domain Attractive salary package available Location: Kensington campus in NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) 2-3 days in the office About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 50 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Lead Integration Engineer (Student) is a senior role within the Student and Research (StARs) Domain accountable for leading the design, delivery and operation of enterprise class integration service and leadership for the Student Sub-Domain. The role is responsible for the data and technical architecture, design, delivery, and lifecycle management of complex integration solutions, primarily leveraging established technologies and platforms such as AWS, Azure and PeopleSoft Integration Broker. The Lead Integration Engineer has a leading role in the research, analysis and adoption of emerging technologies and platforms to support future integration needs. Operating with a high degree of autonomy, the role acts as the authoritative subject-matter expert for integration, shaping strategy, setting standards, mentoring staff, and ensuring secure, scalable, and reliable student data exchange across enterprise, cloud, and third-party systems. The role demonstrates end-to-end ownership of the Student Integrations services and platforms, influencing long-term architectural direction and enabling UNSW’s technology strategy. The Lead has responsibility for a team of employees, contractors and vendors who deliver the Student Sub-Domain integration services. The position reports to the Head of Student Solutions. Specific accountabilities for this role include: Provide enterprise-level leadership for data and integration architecture across the Student Sub-Domain suite of services and products. Manage and govern data and integration standards, patterns, and best practices aligned to UNSW enterprise architecture and integration strategies for the subdomain. Act as the senior technical authority and escalation point for complex integration, performance, and security issues. Lead the design and delivery of highly complex, mission-critical integration solutions for the subdomain. Ensure integration solutions meet performance, scalability, availability, and security requirements. Review vendor integration designs and technical proposals to ensure they meet UNSW standards and business needs. Monitor the ongoing performance and operation of integrations to ensure they remain efficient and fit for purpose. As part of the lifecycle management of our integration solutions, develop plans to evolve legacy integrations to more mature architectures and platforms, reduce technical debt and leading to greater consistency across all our integration services. Partner with cloud, infrastructure, security, integration, architecture, and other teams to implement appropriate solutions, monitoring, and security controls. Oversee the development and maintenance of high-quality documentation, standards, roadmaps, and operational runbooks. Work in close partnership with senior stakeholders, functional leads, architects, central integration team, and vendors to define integration requirements and outcomes. Provide technical leadership and expert advice on integration strategy, risk, and technology direction. Influence architectural and delivery decisions beyond own team, contributing to enterprise technology roadmaps. Provide technical leadership, mentoring, and coaching to the team. Drive feedback loops and retrospectives across teams to refine processes, remove barriers, and enhance delivery performance. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values and ensure hazards and risks psychosocial and physical are identified and controlled for tasks, projects, and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. Who you are: Tertiary qualification in Information Technology (or equivalent), with demonstrated success managing complex integration and applications in large, diverse organisations. Proven advanced experience leading the delivery and operation of enterprise-scale integration services. Experience integrating large ERPs (preferably PeopleSoft), CRMs (preferably Dynamics 365) with SaaS and cloud-native integration platforms. Deep knowledge of Student Information Systems and their associated integration frameworks and standards. Strong understanding of typical data sets for key business processes across the entire student academic lifecycle Demonstrated experience with the design and development of API-based and event-drive integration architectures. Experience with DevOps practices, CI/CD pipelines, and automation. Relevant cloud certifications (AWS or Azure) Experience operating within large, federated, or higher education organisations. Expert knowledge of integration security and hybrid architectures. Demonstrated ability to lead complex technical initiatives with minimal supervision. Demonstrated ability to build and manage high performing teams. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: please apply through the portal, we would like you to submit a full application including resume and addressing the who you are section. Applications close: Monday 5th of October at 11.30pm Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Jen MacLachlan j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Partner – UNSW IT Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 05 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/541872/postdoctoral-fellow-biostatistics-and-cancer-epidemiology": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Fellow (Biostatistics and Cancer Epidemiology)",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Fellow (Biostatistics and Cancer Epidemiology) Apply now Job no: 541872 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate, Senior Research Associate Post-Doctoral Fellow ( Biostatistics and Cancer Epidemiology ) Employment Type: Full - time (35 hours a week) Duration: 2-Year Fixed Term (possible extension, funding dependant) Remuneration : Academic Level B $133k - $156k base (+17% super, plus leave loading) Work rights: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position Location: UNSW South-West Sydney Clinical School, Liverpool Hospital (Hybrid working) UNSW School of Clinical Medicine The School of Clinical Medicine encompasses all our clinical campuses and disciplines as well as several other affiliated clinical sites. We have four main metropolitan clinical campuses which include our South West Sydney Campus, St George and Sutherland Campuses, the Randwick Campus and St Vincent's Healthcare Campus. Our Rural Campuses include Port Macquarie, Coffs Harbour, Wagga Wagga, Albury and Griffith. The Opportunity This will be in a research-focused position working within the Collaboration for Cancer Outcomes, Research and Evaluation (CCORE), Ingham Institute, South-West Sydney Clinical School, UNSW. They will have the opportunity to refine expertise in health services and equity research and contribute to the University's standing as a national leader in the discipline. The Post-Doctoral Fellow will have the opportunity to work on statistical analyses of large-scale administrative datasets examining inequalities in evidence-based care for cancer. The research will involve data linkages of Australian Census and other Commonwealth health and socioeconomic datasets to NSW health data. This is a unique opportunity to work within a multidisciplinary team alongside clinicians and staff specialists who provide practical, real-world insights and expert guidance to support the interpretation and analysis of complex data. Associate Professor Mei Ling Yap Dr Md Mijanur Rahman Key skills and experience required: A PhD in Biostatistics, Epidemiology, Data Science, or related discipline and/or relevant work experience Have contributed to competitive grant-funding applications. An interest in research aimed at reducing health system inequalities and improving cancer outcomes. High level statistical and data analysis skills in advanced analytic methods including causal inference and prognostic/risk prediction modelling. It will be essential to have evidence of strong data management skills and experience managing large linked administrative datasets using statistical software including Stata, R or SAS, python. Have an emerging publication track record with high impact publication(s) within biostatistics, cancer and health services research or another related field. Ability to engage with a variety of audiences, tailoring communication to suit the relevant audience. Full details can be found in the position description. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during Christmas holidays Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. In your application, please provide a resume and a separate cover letter addressing the key skills and experience for the role listed in the Position Description. Please also list your research publications relevant to this role. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Also note that the successful incumbent will also need to undergo NSW Health pre employment checks in addition to the UNSW checks. Enquiries: Merita O'Connell, Talent Acquisition Partner E: merita.oconnell@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 30 th September 2026, before 11.30pm Sydney time. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 30 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542152/lead-business-analyst": {
      "title": "Lead Business Analyst",
      "description": "Lead Business Analyst Apply now Job no: 542152 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management Employment Type: Full Time, Fixed Term, 2-year opportunity Remuneration: Competitive Total Remuneration Package Location: Kensington-based role with frequent travel to Canberra. About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. About the Role: Reporting to the Program Manager for the UNSW Canberra City Program, this is an exciting opportunity for an experienced Lead Business Analyst with a background in designing and implementing operating model evolution, aligning localised operational needs with enterprise-wide standards. UNSW is expanding in Canberra from a University College supporting Defence (ADFA) to support broader enterprise activities requiring change in the ways of working across multiple sites, a larger spectrum of education offerings, new types of partnership and collaborator engagements and a broader range of student profiles. As the geopolitical landscape evolves and the university plays its part, this role will also help translate the university’s security posture to processes, policies, systems and people models. Responsibilities Lead the end-to-end design of the Canberra City Precinct workforce and input into broader University operating model work by translating business architecture into Canberra specific frameworks for governance, process, technology, and performance. Conduct current-state assessments, gap analyses, and future-state modelling to evaluate feasibility, assess risks, and deliver evidence-based recommendations and business cases. Facilitate co-design workshops and engage cross-functional stakeholders across faculties, divisions, and precinct partners to balance local requirements with enterprise standards. Drive cross-functional collaboration and provide leadership to direct and indirect teams, creating clarity, accountability and opportunities for capability development. Provide strategic advice, facilitation and coaching to support leaders and teams through significant organisational change. Contribute to systems, processes and tools that improve operating model effectiveness and strengthen organisational capability. Lead local alignment of operating model and organisational design initiatives that enable strategic transformation and improved business performance. Support governance bodies and steering committees by defining KPIs, establishing reporting dashboards, and building continuous improvement mechanisms to monitor operational performance. Partner with change management teams to develop stakeholder impact matrices, communication plans, and executive presentation packs that facilitate project rollouts. About You Tertiary qualifications and extensive industry experience, including at least 5 years leading business analysis teams and 7+ years in operating model design or enterprise workforce transformation within complex organisations. Advanced expertise in conducting operating model reviews, strategic project planning, value stream mapping, and delivering high-quality analytical documentation. Proven consultation, influencing, and negotiation skills with a track record of building trusted relationships across diverse academic, executive, and external stakeholders. A collaborative leadership style supported by strong accountability and commercial awareness. The ability to lead effectively through ambiguity, complexity and competing organisational priorities. Strong analytical, problem-solving, and initiative-driven capabilities to evaluate risks, assess feasibility, and develop robust solutions for complex business challenges. Outstanding written, verbal, and facilitation skills, backed by high proficiency in standard office and diagramming toolsets (Excel, PowerPoint, Word, Visio) for executive reporting. Excellent organisational and time management skills to manage competing priorities, with higher education experience and alignment with UNSW values highly desirable. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before 11:55pm, Monday 5 October 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 05 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542792/educational-technology-specialist": {
      "title": "Educational Technology Specialist",
      "description": "Educational Technology Specialist Apply now Job no: 542792 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Student Services, Analyst Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: 2 x FT Contracts until December 2027 Remuneration: Level 7 from $118k + 17% superannuation & leave loading Location:  Kensington NSW Why your role matters The Educational Technology Specialist plays a key role within the Pro Vice-Chancellor, Education portfolio as part of the Educational Technology Enablement team. The position connects teaching needs with technology by supporting the effective use of educational tools and providing operational oversight of UNSW’s educational technology services and platforms. The role is responsible for implementing, supporting and continuously improving technology-enhanced learning experiences. This includes working closely with academic staff to support the effective integration of tools such as the learning management system, digital assessment, media tools and other learning applications. The position also contributes to platform configuration, testing, documentation and process improvement, ensuring services meet the needs of educators. The Educational Technology Specialist monitors system performance and user feedback to identify opportunities for improvement, escalates issues where required and ensures compliance with institutional and technical standards. Working collaboratively with the Educational Technology Enablement and Educational Excellence teams, the role supports the delivery of portfolio projects and initiatives while maintaining current business knowledge across all team functions. The position plays a critical role in piloting and scaling emerging technologies, working with educators, IT teams, vendors and governance bodies to align technology use with UNSW’s strategic and educational goals. Specific accountabilities include: Enhance Teaching and Learning through Technology: Provide guidance and support to academic staff on the effective use of educational technologies in different teaching contexts in collaboration with the Educational Excellence team as required. Collaborate closely with educators and academic stakeholders to understand their pedagogical needs to ensure education technologies are fit for purpose, scalable and aligned with institutional goals; Champion the continuous improvement of education technologies and user-centred design and adoption. Support Capability Building: Provide support, training, guidance, and resources to academic and professional staff to improve their confidence and capability in using educational technologies effectively ( (e.g., LMS, Turnitin, assessment tools) Support Learning Platforms: Coordinate operational services, vendor collaborations and contribute to the problem solving, effective use, optimisation, and enhancement of the University’s Educational Technology platforms to support system stability, accessibility, and high-quality user experience. About You: A relevant tertiary qualification in a relevant field or an equivalent level of knowledge and experience gained through a combination of education and training. Demonstrated ability to support, guide and provide consultation for educational technologies, including web-based educational and media systems and in the provision of technical help desk support. Demonstrated experience contributing to the design and delivering of high-quality training programs and resources to academic staff, professional staff and students, with a customer-focused approach. High-level problem-solving skills and ability to exercise initiative and autonomy in interacting with and critically evaluating new and emerging educational technologies to enhance services and processes. Strong service orientation and customer focused together with excellent interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to build and maintain positive and professional relationships with staff at all levels and internal and external stakeholders. Advanced level of skills in using various educational technology applications; including exposure to the Microsoft Power Platform, HTML, JSON, XML. Experience identifying and implementing automation, integration, and process improvements using appropriate platforms and technologies. Continuous improvement mindset with the ability to identify opportunities to enhance systems, processes, and user experiences through innovative solutions. Demonstrated capacity for effective project and resource management and experience in participating/ contributing to e-learning projects and initiatives. Excellent organisational and time management skills, including the ability to effectively manage multiple tasks and conflicting deadlines; ability to work autonomously and contribute to the team. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter addressing the key selection criteria (no more than 2 pages) before 11.55pm Thursday 1st October 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Job queries contact: Chloe Gane, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: chloe.gane@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542808/senior-strategy-specialist-cellular-genomics-future-institute": {
      "title": "Senior Strategy Specialist- Cellular Genomics Future Institute",
      "description": "Senior Strategy Specialist- Cellular Genomics Future Institute Apply now Job no: 542808 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Strategy, Clinical/Medical Research Employment Type: Part – time; 17.5 hours per week Duration: 2 years fixed Term Remuneration: Professional Level 9 $153,933 pro rata + 17% super+ annual leave loading Location: Kensington, New South Wales . Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. The Senior Strategy Specialist leads the translation of the Cellular Genomics Future Institute’s strategic vision into operational delivery, organisational capability and sustainable growth. Working closely with the Institute Director, the role drives strategic planning, business development, governance, institutional partnerships and major cross-functional initiatives, ensuring alignment with Faculty, University and broader research priorities. The role identifies and develops new funding, commercial and partnership opportunities across research, healthcare, government, philanthropy and industry, while establishing effective governance and performance frameworks to support the Institute’s long-term sustainability and translational impact. The Senior Strategy Specialist also acts as a key strategic adviser to the Director and represents the Institute with senior internal and external stakeholders. The role reports to the Institute Director and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: Qualifications in biomedical science, genomics, medicine, health, business, innovation management or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of education and senior leadership experience. Demonstrated experience leading organisational strategy, institutional development or major strategic initiatives within research, healthcare, biotechnology or innovation environments. Proven ability to translate strategic vision into operational execution and measurable organisational outcomes. Strong business development experience, including building partnerships across academia, healthcare, government, philanthropy and industry. Demonstrated success securing major funding through competitive grants, strategic partnerships or commercial agreements. Experience developing sustainable business models and evaluating commercialisation opportunities. Experience designing and implementing governance frameworks, organisational policies and executive reporting processes. Exceptional stakeholder engagement, negotiation and influencing skills, with demonstrated political judgement and executive presence. Outstanding written and verbal communication skills, including preparation of Board papers, strategic proposals and investment cases. Experience leading organisational change and complex cross-functional programs in large, matrixed organisations. Demonstrated ability to lead, mentor and develop high-performing professional teams. Experience working within regulated clinical, healthcare or research environments is highly desirable, including familiarity with quality systems, accreditation or governance frameworks. Knowledge of translational genomics, precision medicine, biomedical research or related sectors would be highly regarded. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application including your resume & cover letter addressing the selection criteria, online before Sunday 27 th September at 11:55pm. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, WWCC and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542892/senior-advisor-geopolitical-risk": {
      "title": "Senior Advisor, Geopolitical Risk",
      "description": "Senior Advisor, Geopolitical Risk Apply now Job no: 542892 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Knowledge Exchange Employment Type: Full-time, continuing position Remuneration: Level 8 position: 132k-$149k + 17% superannuation Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid Working) Why your role matters As geopolitical complexities continue to influence the global research landscape, universities continue to navigate complex international partnerships while maintaining the free exchange of ideas that underpins world-class research. As Senior Advisor, Geopolitical Risk, you’ll play a key role in UNSW's National Security Team to support safe, compliant and sustainable international engagement across the University. This is an advisory position which supports researchers to confidently navigate risk and continue international engagement in a rapidly evolving geopolitical environment. Working directly with stakeholders across UNSW, alongside government agencies and external partners, you’ll assess geopolitical risk and provide practical, balanced advice to UNSW management and researchers. Reporting to the Manager, Geopolitical Risk, this role operates at the intersection of international relations, national security and emerging technologies to enable international research collaboration. As Senior Advisor, Geopolitical Risk, you will: Use your understanding of Australian foreign policy and the geopolitical environment to assess a broad range of UNSW’s international activities, to deliver practical advice that enables global research engagement in line with the Australian University Foreign Interference Taskforce Guidelines. Build trusted relationships with academics, researchers, University leaders and external stakeholders. Engage with government agencies and monitor emerging geopolitical, national security and policy developments. Provide guidance on foreign interference, critical technologies, sanctions and related risk frameworks. Support strategic responses to emerging geopolitical and international engagement issues. Escalate complex or high-risk matters while maintaining a pragmatic, solutions-focused approach. Who You Are: We're looking for a strong relationship builder and trusted advisor who is comfortable exercising judgement in an environment where risks are continually evolving. Holding a background in international relations, geopolitical risk consulting or strategic advisory services, you’ll be confident in navigating risk assessments across this field. You will bring: Relevant qualifications and experience in a discipline such as law, international policy, intelligence or international relations, or equivalent skills gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Expertise in national security matters relating to the higher education sector, advanced research environments or sensitive security environments. Strong understanding of Australian foreign policy settings. Demonstrated experience gained working in a strategic advisory or consultancy role with international exposure in a large complex organisation. Experience working with the Australian Government in an international relations or intelligence role would be beneficial Excellent consultation, influencing, communication, negotiating and networking skills. Proven collaborative and team-oriented approach, with the ability to quickly establish and maintain a high level of personal and professional credibility across the organisation. Strong organisation and time management skills. Ability to work on highly sensitive and confidential matters while maintaining integrity and information security. Demonstrated strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to use innovative thinking when developing a robust approach or finding solutions to problems. An understanding of the Foreign Arrangements Scheme, Defence Export Controls, UNSC and Australian Autonomous Sanctions, or Defence Industry Security Program (DISP) is also beneficial. About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. We aspire to be Australia's global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. Benefits and Culture People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff that make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options Additional leave over the December festive period Career development and learning opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses 17% superannuation and salary packaging benefits Access to a range of health, wellbeing and lifestyle programs How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter addressing the ‘who you are’ selection criteria above (1-2 pages) before 11.55pm Monday 28 September 2026. Applications assessed as submitted, we reserve the right to closure applications early in periods of high interest. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application . Job queries contact: Katherine Molloy, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role . UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 28 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542953/work-integrated-learning-officer-faculty-of-medicine-health": {
      "title": "Work Integrated Learning Officer (Faculty of Medicine & Health)",
      "description": "Work Integrated Learning Officer (Faculty of Medicine & Health) Apply now Job no: 542953 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration Work Integrated Learning Officer (Faculty of Medicine & Health) Employment Type: Full - time (35 hours a week) Duration: Fixed Term from 19 October 2026 - 5 October 2027 Remuneration : $108,021 base (+17% super and a leave loading) Work rights: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: UNSW Campus, Kensington (Hybrid working, minimum of 2 days in the office with a chance of those days increasing) The Opportunity The role supports the coordination and delivery of student placements for UNSW Medicine & Health programs and is based in the Education and Student Experience Faculty team. Duties will include identifying placement opportunities, scheduling and allocating placements, maintaining student records and compliance systems, and monitoring placement progress. It works closely with academic staff and internal and external stakeholders to ensure placement requirements are met, while providing clear, proactive support and communication to students throughout the placement process. Key skills and experience required: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience in a tertiary education environment, preferably with organising placements in the Public and Private Health sectors, or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Demonstrated excellent interpersonal and communication skills with the ability to engage with a range of stakeholders including academics, professional staff and professional networks. Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to plan, manage and streamline multiple tasks/processes, respond to changing priorities and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. High-level proficiency in a range of computing skills, including Microsoft suite applications and Placement Management Systems, with the ability to learn and promote the use of new systems. Full details can be found in the position description. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during Christmas holidays Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate cover letter addressing the key skills and experience for the role listed in the Position Description. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Enquiries: Merita O'Connell, Talent Acquisition Partner E: merita.oconnell@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Sunday 27 th September, before 11.30pm Sydney time. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543008/project-manager-id-hfcardiacai": {
      "title": "Project Manager (ID - HF/CardiacAI)",
      "description": "Project Manager (ID - HF/CardiacAI) Apply now Job no: 543008 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management Project Manager (ID - HF/CardiacAI) Employment Type: Full - time (35 hours a week) Duration: 3 - Year Fixed Term (possible extension, funding dependant) Remuneration : $132k - $149k base (+17% super and a leave loading) Work rights: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: UNSW Campus, Kensington (Hybrid working) Centre for Big Data Research in Health The Centre for Big Data Research in Health is Australia’s first research centre dedicated to health research using big data. Our research is collaborative, involving codesign and coproduction methods with consumers, communities and health care providers. Together, we aim to facilitate long term translation and implementation into health policy, service and programs. The CardiacAI research team is comprised of a group of doctors and university researchers who have an interest in healthcare ‘big data’ research that produces rapid, real-world evidence to inform the healthcare of people with cardiovascular disease. The team have established the CardiacAI Data Repository that brings large amounts of Australian healthcare data together in a secure environment with strict conditions for use of these data with an appropriate level of oversight of research activities. This data resource is made available to Australian researchers who have a research idea that will generate new knowledge and increase the quality, safety and efficiency of cardiovascular healthcare. Sharing this data resource reduces the expense associated with accessing these data for a research project, removes duplicatation of this preliminary work and accelerates the delivery of research findings and innovations. The Opportunity The Project Manager will lead the delivery and ongoing development of CardiacAI, a digital health research infrastructure enabling the secure use of electronic medical records, linked health data and AI for cardiovascular research and patient care in NSW. The role will also lead delivery of the MRFF-funded ID-HF project, deploying and evaluating an AI-enabled clinical decision support tool for heart failure care across NSW health services. Operating with a high degree of autonomy, the Project Manager will oversee strategic planning, governance, stakeholder engagement, operations, quality assurance and reporting, while ensuring compliance with UNSW and NSW Health data governance requirements. Working across multidisciplinary teams, the role will build strong relationships with researchers, clinicians, engineers, consumers and health service partners to coordinate delivery and collaboration. The role will also support research enablement through ethics and governance approvals, data access, researcher onboarding and training, and develop effective project documentation, communication and reporting frameworks to support transparent governance, accountability and continuous improvement. Key skills and experience required: Relevant tertiary qualification in health, public health, health data science, biomedical science, health informatics, computer science, information systems, implementation science, project management, or a related discipline, with subsequent relevant experience, or equivalent competence. Postgraduate qualifications will be highly regarded. Extensive project management experience, with an in-depth understanding of the project life cycle and strategic development of project plans, objectives and documentation. Demonstrated high-level experience in leading the end-to-end delivery of complex multidisciplinary projects in the research or public sector. Experience in managing projects involving health data platforms, digital health technologies, software development and/or data engineering will be highly regarded. Demonstrated experience preparing and maintaining human research ethics and governance applications relevant to the collection and use of health data. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply policies, legislation, and governance frameworks relevant to the collection, use and disclosure of health data. Advanced written and verbal communication skills, and negotiation skills with a high level of attention to detail. Demonstrated success in working effectively and collaboratively across diverse multidisciplinary teams and stakeholder groups while managing competing priorities. Demonstrated experience in budget management and resource allocation. High level of experience working with a range of digital systems and applications. Full details can be found in the position description. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during Christmas holidays Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate cover letter addressing the key skills and experience for the role listed in the Position Description. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Enquiries: Merita O'Connell, Talent Acquisition Partner E: merita.oconnell@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Wednesday 7 th October, before 11.30pm Sydney time. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543031/social-media-officer-china": {
      "title": "Social Media Officer (China)",
      "description": "Social Media Officer (China) Apply now Job no: 543031 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Marketing / Communications Employment Type: Part Time (21 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 5, $92,265 - $96,992 (pro rata) + 17% super + leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid work arrangement available) About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. Why Your Role Matters: The Marketing & Digital Experience portfolio has been established to enhance UNSW’s global brand reputation, create sharper brand distinctiveness, amplify UNSW’s globally renowned expertise across societal impact framework themes, and support the university to realise the ambition of its new ’Progress for All’ strategic plan. The Social Media team within the Marketing & Content pillar drives UNSW's social media strategy, covering strategic advice, content creation, editorial planning, community management, and reporting. Reporting to the Social Media Specialist, International, the Social Media Officer (China) plays a key role in supporting the management of UNSW’s Chinese social media communities and delivering engaging, consistent content. Key responsibilities include assisting in implementing the university’s social media strategy for China, creating in-language content with a clear brand voice, managing community interactions, and collaborating with internal stakeholders to deliver high-value content and social media support. Who You Are: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Chinese language proficiency, with a strong understanding of the Chinese cultural landscape and social media ecosystem. Proven experience in supporting a social media strategy – managing communities and creating content for Chinese social media platforms including RedNote and WeChat. Strong content creation and storytelling skills, with experience developing engaging written and video content for digital audiences. Strong knowledge of social media audience behaviour, platform trends and emerging developments across the Chinese digital landscape. Excellent organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and work flexibly when required. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the position description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter (required) detailing your interest and suitability for the job before Wednesday 7th October by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December shutdown period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: For queries on the recruitment process, please contact Rachel Darcy: r.darcy@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contacts above cannot be accepted. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543075/wellness-support-advisor": {
      "title": "Wellness Support Advisor",
      "description": "Wellness Support Advisor Apply now Job no: 543075 Work type: Part Time Location: Port Macquarie, NSW Categories: Student Services Wellness Support Advisor, Rural Employment Type: Part - time (21 hours a week) Duration: Fixed Term contract until 30 th June 2027 Remuneration : $118,483 base pro rata, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: Port Macquarie, NSW UNSW Rural (Port Macquarie campus) UNSW Rural brings world-class medical education to rural and regional Australia, delivering the full 6-year medical course at our rural campuses across several locations. Face-to-face teaching is critical to rural program delivery - as students build strong relationships with and benefit from more personal interaction with clinicians and other academics and become a part of the local medical community. UNSW’s program has strongly supported the aims of the Australian Government Department of Health and Aged Care , Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training (RHMT) program for over 25-years, delivering excellence in rural medical education and student experience. Details of the role The Wellness Support Advisor, Rural provides support for rural students with social, emotional, health and wellbeing needs through triage, assessment and short solutions-based therapy. The position holder works closely with the Faculty Wellbeing Officer and senior academic and professional staff at local Rural Clinical Campus sites, as well as local services such as the Rural Adversity Mental Health Program (RAMPH) and other health professionals. The role will liaise regularly with UNSW Central Services such as Mental Health Connect, Psychology and Wellness, Equitable Learning Service and Student Support Team, as well as the UNSW Medicine and Health community. This position holder has a critical role in supporting students through a positive approach to wellbeing that enhances the student experience. In collaboration with the Manager, Rural, the position holder will develop a work schedule to ensure on-campus availability between Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday each week at the identified campus and attend Coffs Harbour campus to support our student cohorts, as agreed with supervisor and at other times as required. The position reports to the Manager, Rural and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant post qualifying experience in Psychology, Counselling, Occupational Therapy, Social Work, or Mental Health Nurse with registration or eligibility for registration with the appropriate accrediting body. Demonstrated experience working with individuals who have psychosocial support needs such as disabilities, mental health, and chronic physical health, and significant experience conducting psycho‑social needs and risk assessments; and undertaking short‑term therapeutic interventions. Capacity to support students to navigate administrative processes in a complex environment, consult and influence stakeholders and advocate on behalf of students. Highly advanced interpersonal and communication skills, with demonstrated ability to empathise and provide specialised advice to all rural students. Proven experience providing high‑quality support programs or service delivery to people from diverse backgrounds. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. A current WWCC is a condition of employment for this role. Other pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Employment history and reference checks Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Current Drivers Licence Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Thursday 1 st October before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-FM1 Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543211/health-and-safety-project-coordinator": {
      "title": "Health and Safety Project Coordinator",
      "description": "Health and Safety Project Coordinator Apply now Job no: 543211 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Risk & Safety Employment Type: Full Time, Fixed Term Duration: 2 Years Remuneration: Level 6 $108,021 - $115,233 + 17% superannuation Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid work arrangement available) About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW is embarking on a significant transformation across its Health & Safety (H&S) function, with a focus on strengthening systems, processes, data insights, risk management, compliance, and continuous improvement across the University. We are seeking a highly organised and proactive Health & Safety Project Coordinator to support the delivery of strategic projects and initiatives that enhance health, safety and wellbeing outcomes for our staff, students and wider community. Reporting to the Health & Safety Manager, Projects, you will support the delivery of projects and initiatives across the Health & Safety function, working across key streams including Systems & Data, Risk & Assurance, Safety Operations, Contractor Safety, and Health & Wellbeing. You will play a key role in helping teams coordinate projects, improve processes, track deliverables, engage stakeholders, and support transformational change across a complex and dynamic environment. As a Health & Safety Project Coordinator, you will: Support the delivery of Health & Safety projects and initiatives across multiple workstreams and the H&S Program of Work. Coordinate project plans, schedules, reporting and administrative activities to support successful project delivery. Monitor project progress, track deliverables and prepare project updates, reports and dashboards. Coordinate stakeholder engagement activities, meetings and workshops, including preparation of agendas and minutes. Collate and analyse data to support reporting, insights and informed decision-making. Provide administrative, procurement and logistical support across the broader Health & Safety function. Support project governance, risk and issue management, continuous improvement initiatives, and compliance with Health & Safety legislation and University requirements. Who You Are: You hold a Certificate IV in Work Health & Safety (or higher), or have equivalent experience gained through a combination of education, training and/or relevant work experience. You have experience supporting Health & Safety activities, including areas such as risk assessments, compliance initiatives, audits, investigations, assurance programs and/or safety management systems. You have demonstrated experience providing project coordination or project support within a complex organisation. You are highly organised, with the ability to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and adapt to changing requirements. You possess excellent written and verbal communication skills, with strong attention to detail and the ability to prepare high-quality reports, presentations and project documentation. You have proven stakeholder engagement skills and can build effective working relationships across diverse teams and functions. You demonstrate strong administrative and problem-solving capabilities, with the ability to identify opportunities for process improvement and deliver effective outcomes. You are proficient in Microsoft Office applications, particularly Excel, PowerPoint and Teams. You have an interest in data, reporting and dashboards, including the ability to collate information from multiple sources, identify trends and support informed decision-making. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the position description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter (required) detailing your interest and suitability for the job before Tuesday 6th October by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture : People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December shutdown period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: For queries on the recruitment process, please contact Katherine Molloy: katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contacts above cannot be accepted. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 06 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543218/senior-program-officer": {
      "title": "Senior Program Officer",
      "description": "Senior Program Officer Apply now Job no: 543218 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management Employment Type: Full-time, Fixed Term 18 month opportunity. Remuneration: Level 7 – from $118,483 + 17% superannuation and leave loading. Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid working model) About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. Why your role matters A Senior Program Officer plays a pivotal role within the Enterprise-wide Portfolio Management Office, driving high-level, practical program and project support for complex transnational education initiatives and key stakeholders. This position leads the coordination of cross-border project activities, business analysis, and current-state documentation in close collaboration with Business Analysts. It is central to building robust internal and external partnerships across global campuses and faculties, driving targeted communications, supporting change management, and ensuring top-tier program governance and delivery outcomes. Reporting directly to the Program Director, the role operates with no direct reports. About the Role You will be required to undertake and have experience in the following areas of expertise: Assist in delivering strategic and operational goals by contributing to project plans, schedules, and planning processes. Monitor deliverables against approved time, cost, and quality metrics, and report on project status to inform decision-making. Drive project governance by facilitating meetings and workshops, tracking action items, and ensuring compliance with established EPMO and UNSW frameworks. Build and manage effective internal and external communications with key stakeholders across the University. Collaborate with Business Analysts to map current-state processes, evaluate risks and improvement opportunities, and deliver strategic recommendations through data and stakeholder insights. Extract system data from front and back ends, summarising findings in a clear, cohesive format. Proactively support change management and training initiatives to address shifts in business processes, system functionality, and requirements. Foster a culture of ongoing improvement by ensuring newly designed solutions are successfully embedded and sustained within the University. Skills and Experience Relevant tertiary qualification combined with substantial project experience or equivalent professional expertise. Demonstrated experience within transnational education (TNE) is highly desired. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, complemented by strong attention to detail. Sound stakeholder management capabilities, with the ability to liaise effectively across diverse teams. Demonstrated experience working with various computer systems, applications, and university administration tools to collect and analyse data. Excellent time management and prioritisation skills, with the ability to work both collaboratively in a team and autonomously using initiative. A solid understanding of the project life cycle, along with a commitment to health and safety responsibilities and UNSW's values and policies. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before Monday 5 October by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 05 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/539298/lecturer-pure-mathematics": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Pure Mathematics",
      "description": "Lecturer - Pure Mathematics Apply now Job no: 539298 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Lecturer One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. THIS ROLE IS LOCATED IN SYDNEY, AUSTRALIA. The School of Mathematics and Statistics currently has more than ninety continuing academic staff and several dozen research staff as well as visiting academics. UNSW is the only university in Australia to be ranked in the top 100 in the world in mathematics and statistics by each of the three ranking bodies: CWTS Leiden, USNews and QS. The School embodies a broad range of research interests in the areas of applied and pure mathematics and statistics. In Pure Mathematics, the School has particular research strengths in Algebra, Analysis, Combinatorics, Geometry, Mathematical Physics, Number Theory, and Quantum Mathematics. The School's research groups are interconnected, with frequent interactions between groups and with other schools and faculties both within and outside UNSW. The purpose of this role is to conduct independent research and deliver excellent teaching in the School of Mathematics and Statistics. A Lecturer (level B) academic is expected to carry out activities to develop their scholarly research in areas that align with the research strengths in the School and professional activities both nationally and internationally and to actively contribute to the School’s teaching activities across a range of undergraduate and postgraduate courses. Women and candidates from other underrepresented groups are particularly encouraged to apply. NOTE: THERE ARE x2 ROLES AVAILABLE. About the role LEVEL B - $133 - $156 plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Continuing Full time (35 hours) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Actively carry out high quality and high impact research in the areas that align with the research strengths in the School with a view to publication in influential and high-impact scholarly journals. Work both collaboratively and independently on research grant proposals and applications to national competitive grant agencies and other funding bodies. Develop research collaborations within the School as well as links with other groups on campus and/or industry, where appropriate. Develop research collaborations nationally and internationally. Actively disseminate research findings at seminars and conferences within the School, the UNSW environment, nationally and internationally. Supervise Honours and Masters Coursework projects; and supervise PhD and Masters Research students. Contribute to the design of programs and courses. Deliver high quality teaching, using appropriate pedagogical methodologies and technologies. This requires teaching both mathematics and a wide range of non-mathematics students and courses at all levels, including Honours and Masters level courses. Actively participate in school activities and demonstrate an ability and willingness to take on leadership roles in the School. Undertake administrative duties as specified by the Head of School and participate in School, Faculty and/or University committees as required. Actively engage with industry and the community to develop significant productive relationships, attract industry funding and participate in professional activities if required. Work collaboratively with peers across the Faculty and UNSW in all aspects of academic endeavour and contribute to mentoring of other staff. Align with and actively demonstrate the UNSW Values in Action: Our Behaviours and the UNSW Code of Conduct. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) PhD or equivalent qualification in relevant mathematical sciences. Demonstrated research ability in an area of pure mathematics which aligns with the research directions in the school. Evidence of effective teaching. Evidence of research collaborations. Capacity to conduct productive research at an international level. Strong publication record in top, peer-reviewed journals. Demonstrated ability to produce high quality external grant applications. Ability to supervise Honours and coursework Masters students. Experience in student supervision including, for example, Honours and Masters and vacation scholar projects. High level oral and written communication skills and ability to network effectively and interact with a diverse range of students and staff. Demonstrated ability and willingness to deliver high quality and innovative teaching and student experience to both undergraduate and postgraduate students. A track record of significant involvement with the profession and/or industry. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Catherine Greenhill E: c.greenhill@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 14 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542832/finance-analyst": {
      "title": "Finance Analyst",
      "description": "Finance Analyst Apply now Job no: 542832 Work type: Full Time Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Finance Finance Analyst, UNSW Canberra Employment type: Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration :  Broadband 6/7, Level 6 from $108,021/ Level 7 from $118,483 plus 17% super Location : UNSW Canberra, ADFA Campus Eligibility: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. About UNSW Canberra University of New South Wales (UNSW) in Canberra has multiple locations in the Nation’s Capital. UNSW Canberra distinguishes itself from other institutions by its commitment to being thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all endeavours. This combined approach is integral to the university's impact and contributes to its recognition as one of the top 20 universities globally, as well as a proud member of Australia's esteemed Group of Eight. Choosing a career at UNSW means embracing an environment where thriving, facing challenges, and engaging in meaningful work are not just encouraged but integral to the university experience. If you seek a career where you can excel and contribute meaningfully, you've found the right place. At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Why Your Role Matters: The Financial Services team is accountable for providing end-to-end finance services to UNSW Canberra. Our Finance Analyst supports the preparation and delivery of financial management activities and undertakes analysis of Faculty budgets and accounts and translate data into advice and guidance to enable reporting and tracking of revenues, expenditures, and other key financial indicators. Accountabilities: • Develop financial models and spreadsheets to provide detailed analysis and meaningful commentary regarding key issue highlighted by monthly management reports. • Provide finance support to academics, maintain an in-depth understanding of research funding, including compliance requirements, spending timeframes and allowable expenditure, to proactively identify, mitigate and manage compliance risks. • Assist in the preparation of reports for presentations as well as monthly and quarterly financial management packs. • Assist in the investigation of financial data to ensure issues, risk and/or compliance breaches are identified. • Support the preparation and delivery of financial management activities including month end, planning, forecasting, reporting and analysis activities. • Undertake financial modelling and scenario analysis to support decision making. • Investigate variances to budget and forecasts to ensure all issues, risks and/or compliance breaches are identified and managed in a timely manner Who You Are (skills and experience): •Relevant tertiary qualification with and completion of (or towards completing) a CA or CPA qualification (or equivalent) and relevant professional sector experience. • Demonstrated experience in management reporting, budgeting and forecasting in a large organisation or a similar role within a group or divisional financial planning and analysis team or management reporting team. • Demonstrated experience in a research accounting environment. • Sound knowledge of computer applications, budgeting, forecasting, and reporting tools and the delivery of online reports including proficiency in Microsoft Excel and its application in financial modelling and analysis as well as computer applications, enterprise systems, and the delivery of reports and presentations. • Demonstrated experience in focusing on customer/client needs in a client focussed workplace with strong communication, interpersonal and stakeholder management skills. Benefits and Culture: UNSW is committed to helping staff balance work-life responsibilities, by providing access to high-quality services, facilities, and flexible work and leave arrangements. Flexible working options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional three days leave during December festive period Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice Free gym access UNSW-wide strategy to focus on Healthy Body, Healthy Mind, Healthy Places and Healthy Culture. Eligibility: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. How to apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Please apply via our online recruitment system. In your application, please include: your CV a 2-page cover letter addressing the skills and experience outlined in the Position Description. In order to view the position description, please ensure that you allow pop-ups for https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au Applications close: Tuesday 29 September 2026 at 11.30pm Get in Touch To discuss the position please contact: Eric Zhang zhenyu.zhang@unsw.edu.au Queries regarding applications - please contact: h.whale@unsw.edu.au (for job related queries only – applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed) Find out more about the lifestyle and benefits when working with UNSW For further information about UNSW Canberra, please visit: UNSW Canberra For further information on living in Canberra, please visit: Living in Canberra UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 29 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542968/lecturer-in-energy-systems": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Energy Systems",
      "description": "Lecturer in Energy Systems Apply now Job no: 542968 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Lecturer The Opportunity The School of Electrical Engineering and Telecommunications in the Faculty of Engineering at UNSW is seeking a Lecturer for a teaching and research role in Electrical Energy Systems and Power Engineering. This role will help strengthen the School’s capabilities in power systems, high-voltage systems, system modelling, simulation and control, while supporting interdisciplinary research across telecommunications, control systems, signal processing and microelectronic systems. Alongside delivering high-quality teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate Electrical Engineering programs, you will supervise Honours and postgraduate project students and contribute to course administration and academic quality assurance. With established strengths across electrical engineering, telecommunications, signal processing, communications, energy systems, robotics and emerging technologies, UNSW offers an exceptional environment for academics seeking to advance their research profile, contribute to interdisciplinary initiatives and deliver outstanding teaching. You will undertake research that advances electrical energy systems and power engineering, engage with industry, government and interdisciplinary collaborators, and contribute to innovation, knowledge exchange and the development of the next generation of engineers and researchers. Position Details Salary : Lecturer (Level B)- AUD $133,065 to $156,864 per annum + 17% superannuation Employment type: Full-time Appointment type: Fixed term, 2-year convertible tenure track (Fixed-term appointment with the potential to convert to continuing employment, subject to a successful review.) Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia Reporting line: This role reports to the Head of School and has no direct reports. About You You are an academic with expertise in power systems or electrical energy engineering, with the ability to contribute to high-quality teaching, research and scholarly activity across Electrical Engineering. You are committed to delivering engaging learning experiences and supporting positive outcomes for students across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. You will bring experience in teaching and learning design, curriculum development and delivery, together with the ability to use educational technologies and online learning methods. Success in this role requires a collaborative and adaptable approach, with highly developed communication, interpersonal and organisational skills. You will have a demonstrated track record of high-quality and high-impact research in power systems, together with the capacity for potential research leadership. You will be committed to supporting students from diverse backgrounds, contributing to equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives, building effective relationships across disciplines, and engaging with industry and professional networks. Experience with industry or work-integrated learning will be highly regarded. About UNSW UNSW is a world‑leading institution recognised for its scale, prestige, and impact. With strong industry engagement and partnerships across sectors, UNSW provides a unique environment where academic expertise translates into real‑world outcomes. The university is home to cutting‑edge research that drives innovation and societal progress, while its excellence in teaching ensures students are prepared to lead in their fields. For academics, UNSW offers an outstanding platform to flourish — combining world‑class facilities, collaborative networks, and a culture of innovation that supports both career growth and meaningful contributions to the wider community. The UNSW School of Electrical Engineering and Telecommunications is one of Australia's largest and most prestigious schools of its kind. Home to more than 1,500 students and over 40 academic staff, the School is internationally recognised for excellence in teaching, research, and industry engagement across electrical engineering, energy systems, telecommunications, signal processing, control systems, microelectronics, quantum engineering, and emerging technologies. For further information about our School, please visit - https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/our-schools/electrical-engineering-telecommunications Skills and Experience A PhD in Electrical Engineering or a related discipline, along with relevant work experience. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated experience and evidence in teaching and learning design, development and/or delivery at undergraduate and/or postgraduate levels in the area of power systems or electrical energy engineering, as well as across the broad field of Electrical Engineering. Demonstrated track record in power systems research, along with outcomes of high quality and high impact. Demonstrated evidence of research excellence as well as the capacity for research leadership. Experience using and/or designing with educational technologies and online delivery methods. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and passion for educational excellence (e.g. relevant discipline based curriculum design and development at a variety of levels and scales). Demonstrated knowledge of industry or experience of work-integrated learning (desirable). Evidence of ability to support and inspire students from diverse backgrounds and support student equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives. A track record of significant involvement with the profession and/or industry. High level communication skills and ability to network effectively and interact with a diverse range of students and staff. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for this position can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW. To Apply: Please click the “Apply Now” button and submit your CV, cover letter and responses to the Skills and Experience. If you have had any interruption in your career, you are encouraged to include a ROPE statement as part of your application. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: For role-specific inquiries, please contact Prof Jinhong Yuan (Head of School) E: j.yuan@unsw.edu.au For questions regarding the recruitment process, please contact Eugene Aves (Talent Acquisition Consultant) E: eugene.aves@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:50 pm (Sydney time) on Tuesday, 27 October 2026 As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options, including working from home, for eligible staff. UNSW provides 26 weeks of fully paid maternity or primary carers leave upon commencement of employment (subject to eligibility). UNSW is committed to enhancing the experience of ECAs by providing a supportive and collegial work environment and a dedicated framework to deliver support services to assist their research, teaching, and career development. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542970/senior-research-associate": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate",
      "description": "Senior Research Associate Apply now Job no: 542970 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Senior Research Associate The Opportunity The School of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering at the University of New South Wales is seeking a Senior Research Associate to conduct research within the Tribology and Machine Condition Monitoring group. In this role, you will develop new vibration-based techniques to improve the detection, diagnosis and prognosis of faults in machine components, working both independently and collaboratively as part of the research team. This position will give you the opportunity to build your research profile, contribute to high-quality scholarly outputs and develop independent research proposals. You will also contribute to competitive funding applications, participate in research collaborations, and support the development of students and emerging researchers through mentoring and higher degree research supervision. Salary, Level B: AUD $133,065 to $156,864 per annum + 17% superannuation Employment type: Full time Appointment type: Fixed-term contract – 12 months Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia About us UNSW is a world‑leading institution recognised for its scale, prestige, and impact. With strong industry engagement and partnerships across sectors, UNSW provides a unique environment where academic expertise translates into real‑world outcomes. The university is home to cutting‑edge research that drives innovation and societal progress, while its excellence in teaching ensures students are prepared to lead in their fields. For academics, UNSW offers an outstanding platform to flourish — combining world‑class facilities, collaborative networks, and a culture of innovation that supports both career growth and meaningful contributions to the wider community. The School of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering that is internationally recognised for its excellence in research and teaching. Our mission is to nurture students to become industry leaders who will generate societal, economic, and environmental benefits. The School is one of the largest and most prestigious schools Australia for thriving research programs and contribution to education excellence in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering, Advanced Manufacturing Engineering, Robotics and Mechatronic Engineering. Our Schools QS ranking for 2025 is #46 globally and the highest in Australia. For further information on our school, go to - https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/our-schools/mechanical-and-manufacturing-engineering Skills & Experience A PhD in Mechanical Engineering or a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. At least two years of experience in a postdoctoral position (or similar) conducting research in one or more of the following fields: machine condition monitoring and diagnostics, vibration analysis, signal processing, modal analysis, experimental vibration analysis, or a related discipline. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated track record in research with outcomes of high quality and high impact with clear evidence of the desire and ability to continually achieve research excellence as well as the capacity for research leadership. A track record of significant involvement with the profession and/or industry. High level communication skills and ability to network effectively and interact with a diverse range of students and staff. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for this position can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW. To Apply: Please click the “Apply Now” button and submit your CV, Cover Letter and Responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Friday 16 October 2026 As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543176/wild-deserts-conservation-field-assistant": {
      "title": "Wild Deserts Conservation Field Assistant",
      "description": "Wild Deserts Conservation Field Assistant Apply now Job no: 543176 Work type: Full Time Location: Other location Categories: Research Infrastructure One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Wild Deserts is an ecosystem restoration project in Sturt National Park, far north-west NSW. The project is a partnership between UNSW Sydney, Ecological Horizons and the NSW Government. The Wild Deserts Conservation Field Officer plays a key role in supporting Wild Deserts’ ecological and restoration project. As part of a multidisciplinary team, the role assists in managing a 35,000 ha area of Strzelecki Desert Dunefields in Sturt National Park, including two 4,000 ha feral free exclosures and a 10,000 ha area intensively managed to supress feral cats for wild releases of threatened fauna. The role is responsible for undertaking a range of critical activities to support the on-ground delivery of the project including infrastructure and visitor facilities maintenance, pest and weed control, ecological data collection and management. The role is based on site at the Wild Deserts Field Station (at the Fort Grey Homestead Complex) in Sturt National Park, 440 km north of Broken Hill (110 km north-west of Tibooburra). The field station is remote and arid, with low erratic rainfall and high summer temperatures. About the role $92K - $104K plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 12 months Full time - 35 hours The role reports to Wild Deserts Project Coordinator and has no direct reports although may be involved in the direction of casual staff and contractor engagements under manager supervision. Specific responsibilities for this role include: Coordinate regular checking and maintenance of the Wild Deserts feral exclusion fences, totalling 50 km (established new in 2018). Including weekly fence checks, scheduled and reactive maintenance following weather events. Provide assistance in the maintenance and upkeep of field station facilities and assets, including buildings, vehicles, tools and equipment. Carry out conservation management of vertebrate pest species control (e.g. feral cats, foxes, rabbits, pigs) and weed control, as programmed in the Strategic Adaptive Management Plan. Assist the Wild Deserts Principal Ecologist and Field Ecologist roles in ecological and pest control research and monitoring. Examples may include wildlife trapping and monitoring, radio/satellite tracking of wildlife and vertebrate pests, remote camera trap monitoring, drone surveys. Maintain and complete basic record keeping, using basic computer programs and applications. Working with the Wild Deserts Project Co-ordinator, provide direction and supervise external contractors providing maintenance and repair services for the site. Work constructively with a diverse range of people, including local park neighbours, staff from collaborating organisations, visiting university students, researchers, tourists and contractors during day to day works. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. Skills and Experience (Selection Criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: An equivalent level of knowledge in rural or conservation land management, gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. Experience in land management, particularly fencing, infrastructure maintenance, pest and weed control. Demonstrated ability in the maintenance of infrastructure and equipment. Current manual driver's license and first aid certificate. Licensed or willingness to obtain firearm licence (and other accreditations) for feral animal control. Accredited, or willingness to obtain, accreditation to operate a range of machinery, equipment and tools (e.g. earthmoving plant, chainsaw, other). Accredited or willingness to obtain accreditation for using poisons or herbicides in feral animal or weed control. Experience or understanding of scientific methods and research. Proficient computer literacy and accurate record keeping. Well-developed interpersonal and written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated experience supporting and contributing to projects and initiatives. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. For informal queries, please see the below contact details. Otherwise, please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed . Contact: Reece Pedler E: r.pedler@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 14 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW aspires to be the exemplar Australian university and employer of choice for people from diverse backgrounds. UNSW aims to ensure equality in recruitment, development, retention and promotion of staff and that no-one is disadvantaged on the basis of their gender, cultural background, disability, sexual orientation or identity or Indigenous heritage. We encourage everyone who meets the selection criteria to apply. UNSW partners with Australia’s leading diversity organisations, networks, and campaigns. Please refer to UNSW’s diversity offerings for further information on our flexible work and leave options, and support for carers (childcare, parent rooms, parental leave). Position Description Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542811/senior-business-analyst-d365-crm": {
      "title": "Senior Business Analyst - D365 CRM",
      "description": "Senior Business Analyst - D365 CRM Apply now Job no: 542811 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: Full Time Permanent Remuneration: $132K - $149K + 17% super (Level 8) Location:  Kensington Campus (hybrid) About the role The Senior Business Analyst (D365 CRM), internally called Senior Business CX Analyst, is a technically focused business analyst role, acting as the product owner to work as the conduit between the business stakeholders and our technical delivery team to ensure that processes and systems meet business requirements. The role is accountable for analysing and defining requirements and functionality for the CRM system, providing system user configuration and administration. This includes implementation/product ownership, data and business analysis, supporting project and change leadership for professional and academic teams, process improvement, and knowledge management. This position will also be responsible for overseeing/advising on the integration with core UNSW systems. The role reports to the Manager, Student Systems (Core Connections) and has 1 direct report. Key accountabilities Define, document and verify functional and non-functional business requirements, including gap analysis, to support effective CRM solutions Undertake systems and data modelling to support process change and improvement initiatives Build an understanding of cross-Faculty, Division and sector contexts to gather requirements and design solutions that meet client needs Develop, coordinate and support user acceptance testing for CRM changes and enhancements Complete project deliverables, including process and system requirements, testing materials and implementation documentation Support project and program management activities across the Insight CRM program and related project streams. Provide change management support, subject matter expertise, training and assistance to help end users adopt new or modified systems Compile and present reports on data and metrics to inform operational efficiency and strategic planning About You We are looking for someone who is aligned with UNSW’s vision and values and who brings: Demonstrated experience as a CRM/IT/technical/business analyst , both for operational and IT oriented processes, ideally in an education environment High level analytical skills with the ability to collect, interpret and evaluate both quantitative and qualitative data to successfully interpret business requirements and design complete and effective solutions Experience using Microsoft Dynamics 365 (D365) CRM an advantage High level interpersonal, leadership and negotiation skills including the ability to interact effectively with a wide client base and communicate and negotiate with external clients Proven ability to develop comprehensive, concise reports and briefings to stakeholders in a timely manner Outstanding planning and project co-ordination skills with previous experience working in an agile environment to deliver projects. Strong MS Office skills, particularly Excel, PowerPoint, Visio and Jira. Proven ability to provide effective customer service with demonstrated capacity to communicate clearly and confidently with business end-users and functional and technical staff Candidates will need to have ongoing working rights – this role is not available for sponsorship. Recruitment process: Please submit your resume as well as cover letter that addresses your motivation and the experience you bring to the role. You’ll also be asked a couple of application questions (you can save and come back to your answers later). Applications close: 28 September 11.55PM . Shortlisting will commence after applications close. Get in touch for any question or support: Anne de Voronine, Talent Acquisition Associate, a.de_voronine@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 28 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543042/finance-support-services-officer": {
      "title": "Finance Support Services Officer",
      "description": "Finance Support Services Officer Apply now Job no: 543042 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Finance Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term contract until February 2027 Remuneration: Level 5: $92,265 - $104,401 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus Multiple positions available About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: Multiple opportunities are available for Finance Support Services Officers within Operational Finance, delivering financial processes and transactional activities that support the day-to-day operations of UNSW Faculties and Divisions. Operational Finance supports UNSW’s core finance systems and transactional activities, including Finance Support Services, Help Desk, Accounts Payable and Accounts Receivable. The team is focused on standardising and continuously improving processes and systems to support financial management integrity and strategy. These opportunities would suit both experienced finance professionals looking to apply their operational expertise in a large and complex organisation, and emerging accounting professionals seeking to further develop their skills in a supportive, high-volume finance environment. The role reports to the Finance Support Services Supervisor and has no direct reports. Responsibilities: Perform broad financial administration duties, including purchase orders, billing, receipting, month-end activities, journals and routine reporting, ensuring accuracy, data integrity and compliance with financial controls, policies and funding guidelines. Build and maintain strong customer relationships and provide guidance, advice and support on purchasing processes, policies and systems. Support continuous improvement of Finance processes by championing change and seeking feedback. Provide reliable, efficient and effective customer service through timely and professional communication. Foster collaborative and productive relationships across Finance to support effective and accurate service delivery. Undertake other duties required to meet customer and Finance needs, commensurate with the role and level. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline and progression towards a CA or CPA qualification is desirable. Demonstrated experience in high-volume operational purchase-to-pay or transactional financial services within a large and diverse business. Demonstrated awareness of financial controls and experience applying policy and guidelines. Experience managing customer expectations and appropriately responding to customer needs. Well-developed communication, interpersonal and stakeholder management skills, with the ability to work professionally with a wide range of internal and external stakeholders in a high-volume environment. Well-developed time management and organisational skills, with the ability to work under pressure and manage multiple tasks with competing deadlines while providing excellent customer service. Working knowledge of computer applications and experience with enterprise financial systems and software, such as PeopleSoft and Concur. Strong attention to detail and the ability to deliver results within required deadlines and quality parameters. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your CV & cover letter online outlining your interest and suitability for the role, via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 28 th of September . A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au (Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal). Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity, preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 28 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543062/development-project-officer": {
      "title": "Development Project Officer",
      "description": "Development Project Officer Apply now Job no: 543062 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Fundraising / Philanthropy Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 12-month fixed-term contract Remuneration: Level 7: $118,483 - $128,233 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Development Project Officer sits within the Development team partnering with the faculties of Law & Justice, Arts, Design & Architecture and the UNSW Business School. The role maximises the capacity of frontline fundraisers by coordinating and delivering high-quality, portfolio-specific donor communications, stewardship, cultivation and engagement activities, alongside fundraising operations that strengthen donor relationships and maximise the effectiveness of UNSW’s Development team. Working independently and collaboratively with professional and academic colleagues, you will manage a diverse range of projects and complement support provided by the Development Coordinator, Advancement Services and Advancement Programs teams. The role reports to the Head of Development and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Develop high-quality donor communications and materials, including proposals, impact reports, stewardship updates and acknowledgements, in collaboration with Development colleagues and academic staff. Prepare clear and concise internal briefings, talking points and background materials to support donor and prospect engagement by Development Managers, Deans and senior University stakeholders. Coordinate stewardship and engagement activities across team portfolios, including donor reporting, scholarship stewardship, donor visits and engagement events. Support fundraising operations, CRM records, reporting, donor research and portfolio data. Independently manage assigned projects, anticipating needs and progressing work with sound judgement, minimal supervision and to deadlines. Partner with the Head of Development and Development Managers to coordinate operational, stewardship and engagement activities that maximise fundraising effectiveness. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant tertiary qualifications and subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Exceptional written communication skills, with experience preparing high-quality proposals, reports, donor communications and executive briefings. Demonstrated ability to build productive relationships, work collaboratively and influence stakeholders across diverse internal and external groups. Sound judgement, discretion and professionalism when managing sensitive relationships and confidential information. Highly organised, with demonstrated ability to manage multiple projects, anticipate requirements, prioritise competing demands and work independently. Experience using CRM systems such as Raiser’s Edge to maintain accurate records, reporting and documentation. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV and a cover letter outlining your suitability for the role in relation to the selection criteria (listed under ‘skills & experience’ on the PD) via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 29th of September. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 29 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543103/senior-reporting-analyst": {
      "title": "Senior Reporting Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Reporting Analyst Apply now Job no: 543103 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Analyst Full-time (35 hours week), 12-month fixed term contract Level 8 from $132K plus 17% superannuation (depending on experience) Hybrid and flexible working arrangements Why this role? As the Senior Reporting Analyst, you will play a key role within the Division of Research and Enterprise, delivering high-level analytical, reporting and technical services to support research administration across areas including research and scholarship funding, Higher Degree Research (HDR), grants and contracts, and ethics and compliance. The role provides high-level expertise in data analysis, modelling and reporting, including the analysis and interpretation of complex data, development of robust reporting and analytical solutions, and delivery of actionable insights to support operational, strategic and evidence-based decision-making. The Opportunity As the Senior Reporting Analyst, you will turn complex research data into powerful insights, smarter decisions and better outcomes for UNSW. In this role, you will lead the development of robust reporting, analytics and data solutions across research publications, funding, HDR, scholarships, grants, contracts, ethics and compliance. Working with stakeholders across UNSW, you will deliver meaningful analysis, modelling and reporting, improve data quality and processes, and use tools such as SQL, Excel and Power BI to support informed decision-making and enhance research outcomes. About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We are a large organisation with a diverse and highly talented community doing extraordinary work. We are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in all we do, and this collective approach is what gives our work impact. It is also why UNSW is consistently ranked among the world’s top 20 universities in the QS World University Rankings and is a proud member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re seeking a career where you can thrive, be challenged and contribute to meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to benefits including: 17% superannuation and annual leave loading Hybrid/Flexible working arrangements Discounts and entitlements Affordable on-campus parking Who you are Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience, or equivalent competence gained through a combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience within higher education or a similarly large and complex organisation, with a strong understanding of research administration, research data and associated reporting requirements. Extensive experience in data analysis, modelling, problem-solving and reporting, using a range of tools and technologies such as Excel, PostgreSQL/SQL and Power BI, with demonstrated ability to translate complex stakeholder, business and strategic requirements into effective analytical and reporting solutions. Demonstrated experience in analysing and interpreting complex datasets, identifying trends and insights, and communicating findings and recommendations to support operational and strategic decision-making. Demonstrated experience in project management and coordination, with the ability to plan and manage reporting, analytics or data-related initiatives, coordinate stakeholders and dependencies, and deliver agreed outcomes within competing priorities and deadlines. Excellent organisational and time management skills, with demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple concurrent activities and competing deadlines, and consistently deliver accurate and high-quality outcomes. Exceptional communication and interpersonal skills, with demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective and collaborative relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders and communicate complex technical and analytical information clearly to both technical and non-technical audiences. To Apply: If contributing to improving the researcher experience is of interest to you, please submit your CV and Cover Letter via the Apply button before 11.55pm, Tuesday 29 th September 2026. As part of the application process, you will also be asked to respond to three short free-format questions designed to help us better understand your experience.  A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Kim Symons, Talent Acquisition Partner e: k.symons@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 29 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    }
  },
